{"offset": 0, "next": 100, "data": [{"citingPaper": {"paperId": "08113bb5db49403ad7a8aeae8b9157521e38d0bb", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.14492", "CorpusId": 295746108}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/08113bb5db49403ad7a8aeae8b9157521e38d0bb", "title": "ReTrace: Distilling Tool-Using Agents into Planner-Free Workflows", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.14492, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-10", "authors": [{"authorId": "2167129808", "name": "Kai-Yuan Hou"}, {"authorId": "2530049575", "name": "Yang-Ping Kuo"}, {"authorId": "2366165063", "name": "Indranil Bhattacharya"}, {"authorId": "98511776", "name": "R. Nama"}, {"authorId": "2530341957", "name": "Arushi Gupta"}, {"authorId": "2065336976", "name": "Carlos D. Castillo"}, {"authorId": "2121359655", "name": "Prathap Ramachandra"}, {"authorId": "2359283689", "name": "Xiao-Fan Jiang"}, {"authorId": "2366163320", "name": "Francesco Carbone"}], "abstract": "Business process automation runs the same Standard Operating Procedure (SOP) again and again on new inputs. Large Language Model (LLM) agents can follow an SOP written in natural language, but they call an LLM to choose every step of every run, which adds latency and cost at scale. We present ReTrace, which turns an SOP and a few logged agent runs into a reusable workflow in which code, not an LLM, selects the next step. The agent's reasoning, the explanation it writes with each tool call, often states why it took an action; the tool calls alone do not. ReTrace extracts the condition behind each step, merges these conditions across runs into a compact rule document, and generates an executable workflow from it. Only this last stage depends on the platform, so rule documents can be compiled for different orchestration platforms. On $79$ procedures from our production platform, ReTrace workflows reach $93.8\\%$ completion, against $96.8\\%$ for the agent, at $24\\%$ lower cost per task. On $\\tau^2$-Bench, they reach $75.8\\%$ task success (agent: $80.6\\%$) and run in $1.3$s instead of $170$s; three rounds of repair on training failures lift $k{=}2$ workflows from $66.7\\%$ to $81.2\\%$. On eight SOP-Bench procedures, ReTrace without repair trails few-shot generation ($80.6\\%$ vs. $83.6\\%$), but repair from labeled training failures lifts it to $87.8\\%$ at $0.40$s per task, compared to $85.5\\%$ in $46.6$s for the agent."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "90850b49c8ce508a5bda677d8db38f8290223935", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.13740", "CorpusId": 295745056}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/90850b49c8ce508a5bda677d8db38f8290223935", "title": "Multi-Objective Scheduling of LLM-Based Multi-Agent Workflows in Cloud Computing", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13740, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-10", "authors": [{"authorId": "2321679866", "name": "G. Ismayilov"}], "abstract": "Large language models (LLMs) have been increasingly composed into multi-agent workflows to accomplish complex and multi-step tasks. As the open-weight models continue to close the performance gap with the proprietary ones, model parameters are becoming a less significant source of competitive advantage in the artificial intelligence market. Yet how these models are efficiently scheduled onto distributed infrastructure such as the cloud, with respect to overall latency, monetary cost and resource utilization, remains largely unaddressed in the literature despite being an increasingly critical problem. Existing agentic workflow frameworks mostly focus on output quality and task success rates over popular benchmarks, rarely accounting for execution dynamics of LLM-based agents such as inference latency, computation power of heterogeneous resources, cloud-specific billing period and resource provisioning overheads. Therefore, this paper formulates multi-agent scheduling in cloud computing as a multi-objective optimization problem that jointly minimizes makespan and cost while maximizing resource utilization. Building on this formulation, we propose MO-SAIC (Multi-Objective Scheduling of Multi-Agent Workflows in Cloud Computing), a scheduling framework comprising four multi-objective schedulers with Non-Dominated Sorting Genetic Algorithm II (NSGA-II), Weighted-Sum Genetic Algorithm (W-GA), a multi-objective adaptation of Heterogeneous Earliest Finish Time (MO-HEFT) and a random baseline (RAND). We evaluate the schedulers from the workflow scheduling, multi-objective optimization and system perspectives to measure their overall performance, quality of their Pareto-optimal fronts and impacts of workflow topology, cloud resource composition and scalability parameters. We report the experimental results with their statistical significance via the Wilcoxon test."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "b8a7f0d7b4bdb20292802143a858e9d2dc96e6ed", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.12068", "CorpusId": 295732419}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b8a7f0d7b4bdb20292802143a858e9d2dc96e6ed", "title": "MIRIX-2.0: Multi-Agent Memory System", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12068, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-09", "authors": [{"authorId": "2359100511", "name": "Wei-Chieh Huang"}, {"authorId": "2261361951", "name": "Wei-Zhi Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2376539245", "name": "Jia-Yan Nan"}, {"authorId": "2261083308", "name": "Peng Xia"}, {"authorId": "2409933062", "name": "Yu Wang"}], "abstract": "Long-term memory lets LLM agents personalize, stay consistent, and reuse what worked before. Parametric memories need training, consolidation can lose details for good, and keeping every record leaves a growing store to search. We present MIRIX-2.0, a multi-agent memory system designed to be practical, accurate, and efficient at once. It is the most accurate of the compared systems on four of five QA benchmarks. It reaches 91.4% on LoCoMo and 72.6% on LongMemEval-S, where it leads the strongest baseline by 14.3 points, and with AutoDream disabled it gains 31.0 and 22.0 points over HyperMem on single-hop and multi-hop document QA. As procedural memory, it yields the highest mean success among the compared memory methods on ALFWorld and WebShop and reaches 49.2% on MetaClaw-Bench, against 44.0% for MetaClaw's skill library. Six typed memory agents keep complete records as the system of record, and a provenance hypergraph indexes episodic and semantic records with n-ary frames that cite their rows. Retrieval runs under explicit budgets, so the evidence a question inspects does not grow with the history. AutoDream merges duplicate anchors outside the write path without rewriting any record, and it distills task sessions into skills. With AutoDream disabled, MIRIX-2.0 answers faster than HyperMem on all five timed benchmarks. It needs no fine-tuning, runs with closed and open models, and its code is public."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "bba8b71b770055e4dd1dfd355669ab5b35657260", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.12552", "CorpusId": 295732372}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/bba8b71b770055e4dd1dfd355669ab5b35657260", "title": "SciMARS: Multi-Agent Recursive Self-Improvement for Closed-Loop Scientific Discovery", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12552, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-09", "authors": [{"authorId": "2289966059", "name": "Wei-Min Wu"}, {"authorId": "2408723450", "name": "Han Liu"}], "abstract": "We propose SciMARS, a framework for recursive task-local adaptation of multi-agent harnesses in closed-loop scientific discovery. Multi-agent systems coordinate analysis, experimental design, and verification as scientific evidence evolves. However, two problems limit their effectiveness: (i) without shared memory, agents lose observations and repeat work; (ii) fixed role procedures, handoffs, and memory policies perpetuate mistakes despite retained evidence. We address these problems with a shared evidence graph and separate component editors. SciMARS adapts roles and coordination; SciMARS + Memory adds a third editor for how agents record, retrieve, and receive evidence. Candidate probes guide composition and selection of the next harness. We evaluate scientific discovery through experimental-data replay and a digital twin. SciMARS + Memory resolves 12/18 GPT-6-Astra and 2/18 GLM-5.3 attempts on Autonomous Science Bench, compared with 8/18 and 1/18 for the base multi-agent team. On ScienceAgentBench and AppWorld, it solves 14/38 and 11/20 tasks, compared with 11/38 and 5/20 for the base team. These results support combining shared memory with harness adaptation across scientific and interactive tasks."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "a24460ef4a318e3851732cc16dad1b88bcafb4c0", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.12856", "CorpusId": 295732139}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/a24460ef4a318e3851732cc16dad1b88bcafb4c0", "title": "WorkflowArena: Benchmarking Executable Workflow Orchestration in LLM-Based Multi-Agent Systems", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.12856, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-09", "authors": [{"authorId": "2446282189", "name": "Zheng Jiang"}, {"authorId": "2223703482", "name": "Fang Niu"}, {"authorId": "2529372876", "name": "Bo-Kai Peng"}, {"authorId": "2192842343", "name": "Hou-De Qian"}, {"authorId": "2291312857", "name": "Wen-Yi Li"}, {"authorId": "2410898209", "name": "De-Yao Hong"}, {"authorId": "2291442009", "name": "Ming-Ju Gao"}, {"authorId": "2221943418", "name": "Yi-Zhe Chi"}, {"authorId": "2537322", "name": "You-Jie Zheng"}, {"authorId": "2383097217", "name": "Kai-Sen Yang"}, {"authorId": "143940914", "name": "C. Xiao"}, {"authorId": "1720782061", "name": "Li-Feng Sun"}, {"authorId": "2371219369", "name": "Huan-Ang Gao"}], "abstract": "Large language model agents are increasingly deployed as multi-agent systems rather than as a standalone model operating within a single context. Recent agent harnesses allow the model to instantiate worker agents and to author the executable workflow that coordinates them, shifting the design of system structure from developers to the model at run time. The quality of this organization therefore constitutes a distinct capability, yet existing benchmarks either measure end-to-end task completion or evaluate coordination under structures specified in advance. We formalize this capability as workflow orchestration and introduce WorkflowArena, a benchmark of 145 long-horizon tasks spanning 8 professional domains, from software engineering and formal mathematics to legal analysis and 3D production. Each task specifies an initial workspace and an integrated deliverable composed of interdependent components, which is assessed by a hidden verifier. Agents determine autonomously whether and how to delegate, and all delegation is recorded, enabling joint evaluation of deliverable quality and the organization of the underlying work. Experiments with nine models show that no single configuration performs best across all domains and that systems exhibit distinct delegation strategies: at high effort, Claude models in Claude Code invoke workflows less frequently but with larger worker groups, whereas GPT models in Codex delegate more frequently in smaller units. When the executing model is held fixed on a challenging subset of tasks, workflows authored by Claude Opus 5.5 substantially improve weaker executors, indicating that workflow organization is itself a measurable determinant of task performance."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "a564ae6b5b5c02da2e07027a825e6780279e7d3f", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.13144", "CorpusId": 295745967}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/a564ae6b5b5c02da2e07027a825e6780279e7d3f", "title": "Teaching the Harness: Student-Aware Skill Transfer for LLM Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.13144, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-09", "authors": [{"authorId": "2530343697", "name": "Chang Liu"}, {"authorId": "2312177253", "name": "Bo Wu"}], "abstract": "Effective teaching requires reasoning about the learner as well as the task, a capacity that Theory of Mind describes as inferring another agent's latent state from its behavior. We ask whether a stronger LLM teaches a fixed student better when it applies this principle. The student is an LLM agent acting through a harness that fixes its tools, context, and action budget, so a procedure that helps one model can exhaust the budget of another. We introduce Student-Aware Teaching (SAT). The teacher builds a learner model that pairs an empirical profile with hypotheses about the student's limitations, tests these hypotheses with diagnostic probes, and writes reusable skills for the diagnosed failures. A skill enters the library only after it raises success on matched development tasks. With Claude-Sonnet-5 teaching DeepSeek-V4-Flash, SAT reaches 86.46% task success across four primary runs, leading both a profile-free teacher with the same framework (81.04%) and the untaught student (75.00%) in each run. Removing the analytical learner model lowers both prediction and success, and under misleading learner profiles none of the 48 candidate skills passes admission. When DeepSeek-V4-Flash and Gemini-3.8-Flash exchange libraries, each performs better with its own. Saved libraries keep average gains of up to 11.1 points after the teacher leaves. The benefit depends on a student that can follow instructions and a deployment route that delivers them."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "61e3aac6cf6823f34b1e02154a88461b119c8494", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.10787", "CorpusId": 295731441}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/61e3aac6cf6823f34b1e02154a88461b119c8494", "title": "Trustworthy Recursive Self-Improvement with Statistical Guarantees", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10787, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Mathematics", "Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-07", "authors": [{"authorId": "2529847537", "name": "Zi-He Gao"}, {"authorId": "2283261562", "name": "Zheng-Ling Qi"}, {"authorId": "2288441105", "name": "Yi-Fan Cui"}], "abstract": "Recursive self-improvement offers a promising path toward AI agents that improve through experience by refining their own harnesses without retraining their underlying large language models (LLMs). Making this self-improvement process reliable, however, requires more than generating new updates: agents must decide which changes are worth keeping. A greedy gate that accepts updates based solely on higher validation scores can mistake an observed gain for genuine progress and lead to performance degeneration, whereas overly conservative statistical gates may reject beneficial updates and hinder performance improvement. We introduce Trustworthy Recursive Updates with Statistical Testing (TRUST), a framework that adapts an online multiple hypothesis testing procedure to control the rate of false evolution across rounds, with an LLM analyzing historical context to help determine how much evidence is required to update the incumbent. Under interpretable assumptions, we prove that TRUST limits the proportion of accepted updates that result in unacceptable performance loss. We further derive a lower bound on the final performance gain. Across the benchmarks HotpotQA, DROP, and APPS with the models DeepSeek-V4-Flash and Qwen-3.8-Flash, TRUST reduces the average ratio of accepted inferior updates from the greedy gate's 45.59% to 2.78%, while achieving higher final scores than the state-of-the-art statistical gate baselines for recursive self-improvement. These findings demonstrate that TRUST strikes a balance between statistical reliability and performance gain in recursive self-improvement."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "d49d8b6106ae7ad7f355c4b4f72947feb5c10e70", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.09254", "CorpusId": 295660934}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d49d8b6106ae7ad7f355c4b4f72947feb5c10e70", "title": "TwinHash: Two-Sided Object Identity Management for Time- and Energy-Efficient Agentic Workflows", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09254, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-06", "authors": [{"authorId": "2000812896", "name": "Sang-Bu Yun"}, {"authorId": "2047933197", "name": "Jung Gyu Min"}, {"authorId": "2142166025", "name": "Jin Hyun"}, {"authorId": "2528619206", "name": "Jeonghwan Ahn"}, {"authorId": "2171738794", "name": "Youngjoo Lee"}], "abstract": "Agentic artificial intelligence (AI) services interleave large language model (LLM) generation with external tool execution, and increasingly rely on search-based workflows that repeatedly revisit shared reasoning states and computations. These patterns create substantial redundancy across tool outputs, LLM responses, and key-value (KV) prefixes, yet existing serving systems primarily optimize shared prompt prefixes, while naive input-keyed caching is unsafe when identical requests produce different outputs. We present TwinHash, a unified object management architecture that assigns each object an input-side hash key to recognize repeated requests and an output-side content fingerprint to validate and locate stored state, complemented by type-specific reuse guards that preserve correctness and response diversity. A software object identity table maintains reusable objects across requests and trajectories, while a hardware object placement table tracks their residency across hierarchical memory tiers so that evicted state can be retrieved rather than recomputed. Across seven agent frameworks and five benchmark suites, reuse follows workflow structure more than benchmark identity. TwinHash serves up to 81% of tool outputs wherever deterministic tool reads recur, and up to 92% of LLM responses in search-based frameworks. Retrieving an evicted KV-prefix from DRAM reduces TTFT by up to 5.6x on Qwen3-32B, with greater gains projected for larger dense models. Under search-based frameworks, TwinHash reduces end-to-end wall-clock time by up to 87% and measured task energy by up to 81% over a prefix-cached baseline, while a per-key serving cap controls the quality-reuse trade-off in adaptive search. These results demonstrate that unified identity, safety, and placement management can convert structural redundancy in emerging agentic workflows into substantial serving-efficiency gains."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "1475dc36f2f28adf20cd5a79875c3919cf3215ea", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.09218", "CorpusId": 295660739}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/1475dc36f2f28adf20cd5a79875c3919cf3215ea", "title": "BOXBOX: Recursive Harness Self-Improvement for Long-Running Performance Optimization", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.09218, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-06", "authors": [{"authorId": "2224203011", "name": "Yu-Hao Tan"}, {"authorId": "2158452257", "name": "Han-Yun Jiang"}, {"authorId": "2261394438", "name": "Fangkai Yang"}, {"authorId": "2254287436", "name": "Yuan Yao"}, {"authorId": "2267520498", "name": "Taolue Chen"}, {"authorId": "2007693904", "name": "Bo Qiao"}, {"authorId": "2110042497", "name": "Yu Kang"}, {"authorId": "2163383329", "name": "Lu Wang"}, {"authorId": "2007757792", "name": "Pu Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2327998963", "name": "Xiao-Xing Ma"}, {"authorId": "2303522901", "name": "Qing-Wei Lin"}], "abstract": "Automated harness optimization improves agents by learning from trajectories and task feedback. Existing offline methods use reference answers to optimize harnesses for reuse on held-out tasks. However, many real-world tasks are open-ended, while long runs expose new difficulties that offline-optimized harnesses may not address. To study harness optimization in these settings, we introduce PerfRace, a benchmark for long-running performance optimization of software projects, and investigate whether agents can use task-specific experience to recursively improve their harnesses during an ongoing task. To this end, we introduce BOXBOX, which enables agent-triggered harness revision and kernel-managed hot reloading while preserving execution context. We evaluate BOXBOX on PerfRace under matched test-time budgets, comparing it with Frozen Harness, offline Harness Evolution, and Harness Scaling. BOXBOX achieves higher speedup with more efficient use of task time and model cost, reaching a Speedup@4h of 1.70x, compared with 1.54x for the strongest baseline."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "975e721203aaf1cfb1e47467e7856e1c93b23921", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.07495", "CorpusId": 295593499}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/975e721203aaf1cfb1e47467e7856e1c93b23921", "title": "Learning Multi-Agent Orchestration:Higher-Order Interactions in Policy Improvement", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.07495, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-05", "authors": [{"authorId": "2364198578", "name": "Kai Liu"}, {"authorId": "2325728161", "name": "Guo-Jun Xiong"}], "abstract": "Learned orchestration has emerged as a promising paradigm for large language model (LLM)-based multi-agent systems, in which a central orchestrator selects agents and controls the information they receive throughout a task. Despite rapid empirical progress, existing methods train the orchestrator by crediting each decision with its own advantage, implicitly assuming that the other decisions in the episode stay fixed. This assumption fails in orchestration, where the contribution of each decision to the final outcome depends on the decisions preceding it, so the gain of a joint update is not the sum of its per-decision effects. To address this challenge, we introduce \\textbf{IAPO}, an interaction-aware policy optimization framework that explicitly accounts for interactions among decisions. Specifically, IAPO exactly decomposes the performance difference between two policies into the effects of individual decisions and their interactions, which we prove can account for half of the attainable gain, and then optimizes both from sampled trajectories while certifying that every update is an improvement. On long-input versions of GSM8K, MATH, DROP, MBPP and HumanEval, against fifteen published multi-agent and workflow-search systems on the same 7B executor, IAPO's deployed plans reach the strongest system's accuracy with fewer tokens, and removing the interaction term costs 7 to 17 points of accuracy on code."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "04d2ffda334877a42c714256c83f20b7b571e667", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.07624", "CorpusId": 295594843}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/04d2ffda334877a42c714256c83f20b7b571e667", "title": "Beneath the Surface: What We Overlook When We Talk About Agent Evolution", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.07624, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-05", "authors": [{"authorId": "2297250786", "name": "Xiaobo Wang"}, {"authorId": "2282562956", "name": "Zi-Mu Wang"}, {"authorId": "2330241161", "name": "Si-Yu Ye"}, {"authorId": "2265797420", "name": "Jia-Qi Li"}, {"authorId": "2526809778", "name": "Min Tang"}, {"authorId": "2505784112", "name": "Jun-Bo Bai"}, {"authorId": "2279412470", "name": "Yu-Ling Shi"}, {"authorId": "2512656473", "name": "Ruo-Bing Wang"}, {"authorId": "2518068418", "name": "Jing-Jing Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2356588864", "name": "Chao-Fan Wang"}, {"authorId": "2286835859", "name": "K. Wu"}, {"authorId": "2339259770", "name": "Meng-Nan Qi"}, {"authorId": "2341532207", "name": "Kai Cai"}, {"authorId": "2374416817", "name": "Xiao-Dong Gu"}, {"authorId": "2525002153", "name": "Qing-Yu Liu"}, {"authorId": "2266461235", "name": "Zi-Long Zheng"}], "abstract": "Agent harness evolution enables recursive self-improvement (RSI) without updating model weights. We examine whether higher benchmark scores yield reliable, efficient, and transferable improvement. We evaluate four methods across harnesses, general and coding tasks. Repeated evaluations, controlled agent expansion, component interventions, and admission replays reveal recurring patterns. All sixteen evolved agents gain previously uncovered tasks while losing previously covered ones. In one general-task case, reliability rises by 5.2 points despite losing ten previously covered tasks. Most reliability gains also increase deployment cost. Specific changes usually outperform generic reminders, but their benefits can change when combined. The highest-scoring agent does not yield the largest mean improvement in any of our four pools, and stricter admission can discard useful alternatives. Transfer selectively preserves gains: coverage usually falls on the general-task target across both harness types and paradigms, but rises on the coding target with the outer-layer-only harness. These findings motivate preservation-aware evaluation, tested decision rules, and explicit applicability conditions. As a constructive follow-up, EvoAudit turns these diagnostics into revisions of select, analyze, mutate, and gate, achieving the best performance."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "fea3d2023e135dcedc2d4dc822d0dccf8e1e9e50", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.08723", "CorpusId": 295660144}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/fea3d2023e135dcedc2d4dc822d0dccf8e1e9e50", "title": "Divide, Diagnose, and Compose: Optimizing Agentic Pipelines and Harnesses", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.08723, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-05", "authors": [{"authorId": "2218039205", "name": "Divij Handa"}, {"authorId": "2265677478", "name": "Christopher Tensmeyer"}, {"authorId": "2374405379", "name": "Jihyung Kil"}, {"authorId": "2391607869", "name": "Vlad I. Morariu"}], "abstract": "Building agentic pipelines that achieve high task performance while keeping cost and latency low requires jointly optimizing heterogeneous and coupled design choices, including prompts, topology, model selection, and tool policies. Manually searching this combinatorial design space is expensive and time-consuming, while existing automated optimizers typically operate within a predefined subset of this space, modify the pipeline monolithically, or provide little structured evidence for pipeline changes. To mitigate these challenges, we introduce the Decomposed Credit-Assignment Framework (DeCAF), which adapts the design space to the given benchmark and pipeline, optimizes each dimension separately, and generates reasoning traces for every decision. Given a task specification, benchmark examples, and a seed pipeline, DeCAF identifies relevant optimization dimensions and generates diverse experimental hypotheses for each. Independent coding-agent sessions then optimize each dimension under a restricted edit scope, jointly evaluating performance, inference cost, and latency while recording all hypotheses, edits, and outcomes of each experiment. A final synthesis session combines compatible findings across all dimensions and uses a component-level trajectory evaluator, which derives role-specific rubrics from each agent's instructions, to localize underperforming components and guide cross-dimension revisions. Across a diverse suite of agentic benchmarks, DeCAF improves average task performance by 2.58 percentage points, while reducing inference cost by 4.30% and end-to-end latency by 42.45% compared to the best overall baseline."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "c8787c68818e163546244093dc32b663246d5610", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.06254", "CorpusId": 295589254}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c8787c68818e163546244093dc32b663246d5610", "title": "HarnessFlow: MCTS-Guided Harness Optimization for LLM Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.06254, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-04", "authors": [{"authorId": "2455191447", "name": "Zhi-Cong Hong"}, {"authorId": "2452524953", "name": "Qian-Long Wang"}, {"authorId": "2228362755", "name": "Hong-Chen Wei"}, {"authorId": "2467017313", "name": "Ai-Dan Wu"}, {"authorId": "2338066707", "name": "Dao-Hong Ying"}, {"authorId": "2343658051", "name": "Ming-Ze Kong"}, {"authorId": "2382941490", "name": "Bei Liu"}, {"authorId": "2249532171", "name": "Chong Luo"}], "abstract": "The performance of large language model (LLM) agents depends on both the underlying model and the harness that governs interaction with tools and the environment. Yet harness design remains largely manual, and automating it requires navigating an open-ended search space through costly end-to-end evaluations. We introduce HarnessFlow, a framework for optimizing complete executable harnesses under a limited candidate-evaluation budget, with the model and task environment held fixed. HarnessFlow organizes candidates and their evaluation histories in a refinement tree, using a Monte Carlo Tree Search (MCTS)-based policy to balance exploitation and exploration. A coding agent refines selected harnesses using archived code, execution feedback, and a library of reusable harness mechanisms. With 15 refinement attempts per benchmark, HarnessFlow achieves the highest mean test scores among the compared methods on six benchmarks spanning agentic tasks, coding, and web interaction. The optimized harnesses achieve an average score of 69.48%, outperforming the initial harnesses and Meta-Harness by 10.89 and 3.95 percentage points, respectively, while incurring lower average per-task execution costs than hand-engineered baselines. These results suggest that structured harness search offers a promising route toward automating the design of capable and efficient LLM agents."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "69ed51ce722ef5d885d4716dd159d6b2b5423a44", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.05953", "CorpusId": 295588263}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/69ed51ce722ef5d885d4716dd159d6b2b5423a44", "title": "Graph-Mediated Large Language Model Systems in the Agentic Era: Reasoning, Knowledge, Retrieval, Execution, and Coordination", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.05953, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-04", "authors": [{"authorId": "2526730858", "name": "Hai-Hui Liu"}, {"authorId": "2512037987", "name": "Yang-Tao Chen"}, {"authorId": "2425176013", "name": "Zi-Cen Qiu"}, {"authorId": "2524427107", "name": "Yun-Bo Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2298435503", "name": "Jia-Lun Zhong"}, {"authorId": "2155483063", "name": "Wen-Hua Wang"}, {"authorId": "32732179", "name": "Yanzeng Li"}, {"authorId": "2375041858", "name": "Tian Wang"}], "abstract": "Graphs play an increasingly important role in large language model (LLM) systems, supporting structural reasoning, knowledge grounding, retrieval, and memory. As these systems evolve toward the agentic era, graphs also organize execution dependencies and multi-agent communication, linking intermediate observations and retained information to subsequent decisions. However, understanding their contribution requires distinguishing the function of each graph operation, the component responsible for its computation, and the subsequent use of its output. This survey introduces an operation-level role--locus taxonomy for reasoning, knowledge and evidence, retrieval and memory, execution, and coordination. We compare how graph observations and external computations guide decisions, how memory representations and updates support later tasks, and how dependencies constrain execution and information delivery. These comparisons distinguish improvements in local metrics from downstream task performance while retaining the conditions under which results were reported. We then organize benchmarks, comparison designs, process diagnostics, and system costs into an evaluation framework. Finally, we identify open challenges in adaptive computation and stopping, memory maintenance, and dependency-aware recovery. The survey provides a basis for assessing when graph mechanisms are useful and for evaluating their effects across components and stages."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "fee44dc191538760576935f32b89d65a28e103bf", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.10016", "CorpusId": 295732338}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/fee44dc191538760576935f32b89d65a28e103bf", "title": "Pixie: A Context-Aware Multi-Agent Multi-Modal Large Language Models (LLM) Architecture for Digital Marketing", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.10016, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-04", "authors": [{"authorId": "2266406", "name": "V. V. Vichare"}, {"authorId": "2529805993", "name": "Akshat Singhal"}, {"authorId": "118246849", "name": "K. Dubovikov"}, {"authorId": "2529849961", "name": "Vivek Kumar"}, {"authorId": "2355754899", "name": "S. Chaithanya"}, {"authorId": "2368297117", "name": "Saurabh Verma"}, {"authorId": "2123037598", "name": "Humeil Makhija"}, {"authorId": "2529804282", "name": "Mrunal Nasery"}], "abstract": "We present Pixie, a context-aware, multi-agent large language model (LLM) architecture designed to enhance digital marketing operations across real-time and offline activities. Pixie uses a primary orchestration agent alongside task-specific and reconciliation/reviewer agents to support a broad range of marketing tasks. These agents combine zero-shot and few-shot prompting with marketing data, proprietary expert strategy guidelines, and documentation for proprietary statistical models. We experiment with agentic workflows ranging from single-agent to multi-agent systems. We evaluate them using assessments by trained marketing experts and measurements of LLM output variance, focusing on accuracy, consistency, and domain-specific problem-solving. Results show that Pixie's multi-agent framework improves decision quality and operational efficiency in digital marketing workflows. We assess Pixie on common digital marketing processes, including root-cause analysis and reporting, campaign research, and planning. The framework is designed to support additional specialized agents for other marketing tasks and adapt to evolving digital marketing needs."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "2319fdfe8347a7f8f5d52be898ba33012faf58b9", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.05139", "CorpusId": 295508181}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/2319fdfe8347a7f8f5d52be898ba33012faf58b9", "title": "Meta$^2$: Precomputing Reusable Knowledge for Online Harness Evolution in Long-Horizon Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.05139, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-03", "authors": [{"authorId": "2340603961", "name": "Lingzhi Yuan"}, {"authorId": "2526199633", "name": "Yi-Guang Cai"}, {"authorId": "1390516186", "name": "L"}, {"authorId": "2526386878", "name": "Si-Qi Yang"}, {"authorId": "2202174378", "name": "Fei-Jiang Han"}, {"authorId": "2509361763", "name": "Yongkyun Lee"}, {"authorId": "2307917108", "name": "Udari Madhushani Sehwag"}, {"authorId": "2398004564", "name": "Fu-Rong Huang"}], "abstract": "Harness optimization improves long-horizon agents by refining the prompts, tools, memory, and hooks around a frozen model. Offline methods can learn from completed trajectories, but deploy one shared harness that may overfit training tasks and cannot react to evidence revealed during a new run. Online evolution can adapt before, during, or after one run through an evolver, but may have to reason about every intervention from scratch or rely on verifier feedback that is usually unavailable at test time. We introduce Meta$^2$, which moves offline optimization one level up: rather than optimizing a shared harness, it learns reusable knowledge for harness evolution and applies it within each task online. Offline, Meta$^2$ learns a textual Meta-Skill $M$ by contrasting training rollouts of the same task agent under three configurations: without an evolver, with an evolver guided by the current version of $M$, and with an evolver given privileged verifier information available only in training. Contrasts in trajectories and outcomes are aggregated across tasks to refine $M$ into reusable guidance on when and how to evolve, when to leave the harness unchanged, and which interventions to avoid. Online, an evolver combines the frozen $M$ with emerging trajectory evidence to decide whether to KEEP the harness unchanged or EVOLVE it through a task-local edit. Each edit applies to the rest of the same run without resetting its environment or progress. Thus, evolution guidance transfers across tasks while harness edits remain task-local, and held-out verifier feedback is never available to the evolver. On two long-horizon benchmarks, Terminal-Bench 2.1 and DeepSWE 1.1, Meta$^2$ achieves the highest performance among evaluated methods with GPT-5.6-Terra, reaching 81.00% and 44.44%, respectively. Ablations support the value of contrastive evolution guidance and keeping online harness edits task-local."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "c5b97597e3fe6507dcbec833e1693c4798cf04ab", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.04439", "CorpusId": 295507777}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c5b97597e3fe6507dcbec833e1693c4798cf04ab", "title": "DualEvolve: Dual-Level Self-Evolution of Mechanism and Orchestration for Recommendation", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.04439, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-03", "authors": [{"authorId": "2526110114", "name": "Tian-Qi Gao"}, {"authorId": "2373537086", "name": "Chen-Xu Wang"}, {"authorId": "2281378164", "name": "Wen-Xuan Yang"}, {"authorId": "2315311529", "name": "Yue-Qi Mou"}, {"authorId": "2295763603", "name": "Biao Tang"}, {"authorId": "2526353082", "name": "Ming Liu"}, {"authorId": "2326838328", "name": "Peng Yan"}], "abstract": "Recommender systems are widely used in e-commerce and content platforms, where improvement requires repeated model design, training optimization, and experimental validation. Large language model (LLM) agents increasingly automate this development cycle. Sustained autonomous improvement requires agents to improve both recommendation models and their development workflows. However, task-performance-driven approaches select updates mainly by candidates'final scores, without a separate optimization signal for experimentation quality. We therefore present DualEvolve, a dual-level framework that co-evolves recommendation mechanisms and agent orchestration toward recursive self-improvement. Inner evolution revises model and training code using task feedback to improve recommendation performance. Outer evolution scores the inner-loop experimental process, updates harness and skill rules using these scores and execution traces, and adjusts orchestration choices based on next-round scores to improve the quality of subsequent experiments. Experiments on MovieLens-1M and three Amazon Reviews 2023 subsets cover four recommendation methods and 16 settings. DualEvolve achieves higher test means than Base, OpenEvolve, and DeepEvolve in all 64 setting-metric comparisons, with macro-averaged relative gains over DeepEvolve of 7.6% (HR@5) and 9.0% (NDCG@5)."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "09ac7074cc803c86adcdfd3d80593cb399f332b4", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.03618", "CorpusId": 295503160}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/09ac7074cc803c86adcdfd3d80593cb399f332b4", "title": "Strategist: Evolving Reasoning with Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.03618, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-02", "authors": [{"authorId": "2308418220", "name": "Taiming Lu"}, {"authorId": "2326321062", "name": "Qi Xu"}, {"authorId": "2105837204", "name": "Zhi-Gang Hua"}, {"authorId": "2349957935", "name": "Zhuang Liu"}], "abstract": "A model is only half of an AI agent system. The other half is the inference strategy: the reasoning procedure that turns model calls into a response. Today that second half is written by hand, and the experience of building one strategy never carries to the next. We introduce Strategist, a meta-agent that evolves inference strategies across tasks. Each strategy is written as a blueprint,executable code that other strategies can reuse, and together the blueprints form a library. Given a task and a small development set, Strategist retrieves related blueprints, writes candidate strategies, tests them on real samples, and revises them from the results. Every candidate then enters the library, so later tasks build on earlier discoveries. On 30 benchmarks, Strategist beats the strongest baseline on 24 and matches its average accuracy (74.7% vs.74.0%) at 39% lower inference cost. Its strategies also transfer to unseen tasks and other models without further search. The part of an AI system that is not improved on its own now does."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "c4b2449de60cd30e054bc65f3fdc8f34827a1a06", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.02516", "CorpusId": 295502295}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c4b2449de60cd30e054bc65f3fdc8f34827a1a06", "title": "Cumulative and Audited Guardrail Tool Evolution for LLM Agent Safety", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.02516, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-02", "authors": [{"authorId": "2374476648", "name": "Xiao Yang"}, {"authorId": "2257572247", "name": "Ai-Shan Liu"}, {"authorId": "2325884825", "name": "Si-Yuan Liang"}, {"authorId": "2304955104", "name": "Zong-Hao Ying"}, {"authorId": "2341700105", "name": "Le Wang"}, {"authorId": "2109618469", "name": "Jia-Kai Wang"}, {"authorId": "2237942988", "name": "Xiang-Long Liu"}, {"authorId": "2237906923", "name": "Da-Cheng Tao"}], "abstract": "Large language model (LLM) agents increasingly operate in open-world environments where safety risks cannot be fully predefined. However, most existing guardrail mechanisms remain static, relying on fixed rules, prompts, or classifiers that generalize poorly to emerging risks. Here we present EVOLVE, a tool-level self-evolving defense framework that formulates agent security as a process of cumulative security evolution. Inspired by how human societies transform experiences into transferable protective knowledge, EVOLVE externalizes security knowledge into executable guardrail tools that are continuously inherited, independently audited, and iteratively refined through lifelong interaction. Across multiple benchmarks, backbone models, and embodied environments, EVOLVE consistently outperforms existing defenses across diverse risk scenarios with +22\\% average F1 score. We further validate the framework in real-world robotic and autonomous agent systems. Our results suggest that scalable agent safety may require continuously evolving defense systems rather than static guardrails."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "7965af31ed84ecd8674c75b8d982fd340d96d644", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.03178", "CorpusId": 295503623}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/7965af31ed84ecd8674c75b8d982fd340d96d644", "title": "CoworkOS:Scaling Agent Coworkers for Real-world Automation", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.03178, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-02", "authors": [{"authorId": "4376097", "name": "Jiajie Jin"}, {"authorId": "2316562231", "name": "Guan-Ting Dong"}, {"authorId": "2499629513", "name": "Cheng-Jun Pan"}, {"authorId": "2526383219", "name": "Xin Deng"}, {"authorId": "2455322947", "name": "Zhuo-Hui Sheng"}, {"authorId": "2486909710", "name": "Guo-Qiang Wang"}, {"authorId": "2352016379", "name": "Tao Gui"}, {"authorId": "1900406", "name": "Yutao Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2526363415", "name": "Yuxuan Chen"}, {"authorId": "30000816", "name": "Shi-Hong Yi"}, {"authorId": "2365380853", "name": "Bin-Ze Hu"}, {"authorId": "2382833241", "name": "Zhi-Xiong Yang"}, {"authorId": "2495119597", "name": "Zhi-Chao Sun"}, {"authorId": "2375822021", "name": "Rui Wang"}, {"authorId": "2503895275", "name": "Han Han"}, {"authorId": "2526383123", "name": "Ming-Yu Ma"}, {"authorId": "2499651393", "name": "Jun-Hao Huang"}, {"authorId": "2526390285", "name": "Xiao-Tao Wang"}, {"authorId": "2495439365", "name": "Hui Jiang"}, {"authorId": "2398804888", "name": "Yuyang Hu"}, {"authorId": "2302980850", "name": "Xin-Yu Yang"}, {"authorId": "2282543731", "name": "Wanghan Xu"}, {"authorId": "2391873065", "name": "Ya-Jie Yang"}, {"authorId": "2513111", "name": "Zhecan Wang"}, {"authorId": "2269420111", "name": "Honglin Guo"}, {"authorId": "2343324260", "name": "Xiao-Yu Xing"}, {"authorId": "2522801396", "name": "Xiao-Qiang Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2455424509", "name": "Yu-Lun Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2144456832", "name": "Xiaoxi Li"}, {"authorId": "2399060433", "name": "Jia-Hang Lin"}, {"authorId": "2362570366", "name": "Shao-Hua Liu"}, {"authorId": "2042683163", "name": "Shi-Han Dou"}, {"authorId": "2218237934", "name": "Zhi-Heng Xi"}, {"authorId": "2374299112", "name": "Qi Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2394203475", "name": "Yu-Gang Jiang"}, {"authorId": "2241348826", "name": "See-Kiong Ng"}, {"authorId": "2468147446", "name": "Ya-Pan Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2257036129", "name": "Tat-Seng Chua"}, {"authorId": "2186578511", "name": "Ji-Rong Wen"}, {"authorId": "2257039188", "name": "Zhi-Cheng Dou"}], "abstract": "Rapid advances in cowork agents are shifting human-AI collaboration toward real-world automation, delivering software, designs, and other artifacts. However, such productivity work demands both high-quality delivery on complex productivity tasks and continued monitoring and decision-making over long horizons, a combination that remains an open challenge. We introduce CoworkOS, a general-purpose harness for long-horizon work that separates task strategy from execution management: the model decides actions, delegation, and acceptance, while the runtime maintains state and feedback. Its three-layer architecture enables direct execution or delegation to Subagents, synchronously or asynchronously, under stable responsibilities. Through a shared application layer, humans and agents inspect and revise the same evolving documents, presentations, CAD models, and Blender scenes, so human-in-the-loop participation can enter both through conversation and through direct modification of the work itself. To sustain continuity across agents and sessions, CoworkOS further introduces hierarchical workspaces and persistent semantic memory, so working files, key decisions, and long-running Job logs remain shareable, reusable, and resumable after interruption. Across 12 benchmarks and 6 categories of real-world applications, CoworkOS proves highly competitive with frontier agent applications such as Codex and DeepSeek Harness. As a sustained-work case study, CoworkOS uses GLM-5.3 as its backbone to conduct RSI-driven automated post-training on Qwen3.6-35B-A3B. Over five rounds, the model's average score across eight benchmarks rose from 33.19 to 42.95 (+9.76). Looking ahead, we believe CoworkOS opens a meaningful path toward general cowork-agent intelligence."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "388f73531930c3cc384f624d94bbf8bf495da0d4", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.04035", "CorpusId": 295507243}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/388f73531930c3cc384f624d94bbf8bf495da0d4", "title": "Recursa: Toward Recursively Self-Improving Foundation Model Systems", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.04035, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-02", "authors": [{"authorId": "2253476816", "name": "Er-Kang Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2117033724", "name": "Yin Lin"}, {"authorId": "2330462706", "name": "Xiao-Rong Shi"}, {"authorId": "2300408961", "name": "Xuan-Rui Lin"}, {"authorId": "2237499232", "name": "Jing-Ren Zhou"}], "abstract": "Foundation models in changing environments can encounter unfamiliar domain knowledge that their original training did not cover. However, new domains rarely provide large amounts of expert-created training data or reliable supervisory signals for further training. We introduce Recursa, a foundation model system that adapts to new domains by learning from its own experience without human experts or a stronger teacher model. Recursa uses a knowledge repository to store what it learns: domain-specific skills and instructions on when to apply them, along with memories of past task experiences and reflections on what to improve. Recursa bootstraps the repository from domain materials and then refines it through recursive self-improvement. It uses parallel learners to execute tasks and synthesize local improvements, then validates and merges the improvements into the knowledge repository. On Harvey LAB's Firm Knowledge benchmark, Recursa raises the all-pass rate on held-out test tasks from 30.0% to 55.0% with DeepSeek-V4.1-Flash and from 35.0% to 46.0% with Qwen3.8-Flash."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "e27863b1a58125e17ba38efd3c31efbe66dc5807", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "conf/iclr/VuAJRG26", "ArXiv": "2611.01870", "CorpusId": 295408740}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/e27863b1a58125e17ba38efd3c31efbe66dc5807", "title": "Automated Stateful Specialization for Adaptive Agent Systems", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01870, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-01", "authors": [{"authorId": "2525216708", "name": "Myan Vu"}, {"authorId": "2525262818", "name": "Harrish Ayyanar"}, {"authorId": "2526030604", "name": "Jiang Pang"}, {"authorId": "2448063842", "name": "A. Reddy"}, {"authorId": "2525266013", "name": "Mayank Goel"}], "abstract": "Current automated agent design frameworks produce either static workflows that lack adaptability or per-query optimizers that prevent the accumulation of deep, agent-level task expertise. We propose a new direction that reconciles these paradigms: creating stateful teams of specialist agents that accumulate knowledge over time and can be reconfigured for novel tasks entirely without human intervention. To this end, we introduce ASpec, a framework that manages this full agent lifecycle by first autonomously discovering specialist archetypes via evolutionary search and then cultivating their expertise through experience, mirroring how human experts learn through practice and reflection. We further introduce a lightweight hierarchical control policy,\"retain-then-escalate,\"which governs when to leverage the established agent system versus when to adapt its structure. Through comprehensive experiments, we demonstrate that this approach leads to significant performance gains on expert-level scientific benchmarks like GPQA while matching the state-of-the-art on broader domain tasks, demonstrating a promising path toward agent systems that are simultaneously expert, adaptive, and efficient. We will release the code at https://github.com/myanvoos/ASpec."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "b3e80c23dbd8e490f79c10a84ed3fe6c00a67b45", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.01415", "CorpusId": 295502584}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b3e80c23dbd8e490f79c10a84ed3fe6c00a67b45", "title": "Latent Harness: Compiling Agent Behaviors into Post-Block Activation Shifts", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01415, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-01", "authors": [{"authorId": "2310570226", "name": "Ran Li"}, {"authorId": "2345012341", "name": "Shi-Min Di"}, {"authorId": "2374309246", "name": "Ze-Yu Ren"}, {"authorId": "2359688261", "name": "Ling Yue"}, {"authorId": "2275540444", "name": "Jia-Chuan Wang"}, {"authorId": "2275866045", "name": "Lei Chen"}], "abstract": "Harness engineering and self-evolving agents improve language-model agents quickly, yet their gains live outside the model. The harness is re-sent and re-paid on every call, and the standard route to internalization is a weight update that is costly to iterate and fixed once merged. We propose compiling the harness instead. Latent Harness distills an external condition into a rank-8 activation shift. The shift is applied at the output of every decoder block of a frozen backbone, at 0.03-0.05% of parameters. The condition is either an explicit process-level behavior instruction or the behavioral contract of an agentic harness. At inference the compiled bundle carries no harness text and adds no tokens. It exposes three runtime controls for injection strength, injection phase, and shift-space mixing. Compiled behaviors recover the source-harness gain in 8 of 10 preregistered settings. Where the explicit source pays the heaviest per-call token tax, the compiled form exceeds it. The same bundles transfer zero-shot across tasks (5 of 7 pairs positive) and compose in shift space, while stacked text instructions interfere destructively. Used as a search space, they lift budget-matched harness search by 1.45 times. On three agentic benchmarks the operator aligns policy to the harness con- tract. Under matched data, capacity, and effective strength it outperforms same-capacity LoRA, which needs 11.1 times the parameters to reach the same band, and it regresses nothing where the base policy is already strong. Across 1B-to-8B models the method skeleton is invariant while gain locations move with the model-task pair, which delimits where compiled harnesses should be deployed."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "b8fad5f0f454dbb263a925745aaabe78c364c3cc", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2611.01377", "CorpusId": 295503479}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b8fad5f0f454dbb263a925745aaabe78c364c3cc", "title": "A Self-Exploring and Self-Evolving Paradigm for Adaptive Edge AI Development", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2611.01377, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-11-01", "authors": [{"authorId": "2134677112", "name": "Leming Shen"}, {"authorId": "2353439028", "name": "Qiang Yang"}, {"authorId": "2348879370", "name": "Xin-Yu Huang"}, {"authorId": "2348270288", "name": "Zi-Jing Ma"}, {"authorId": "2298902164", "name": "Yuan-Qing Zheng"}, {"authorId": "30601755", "name": "C. X. Lu"}], "abstract": "Large Language Models (LLMs) exhibit powerful code generation abilities, fostering agentic systems to automate edge AI application development. However, existing agents typically operate via fixed agentic workflows and predefined design spaces, struggling to handle rapidly evolving demands of real-world scenarios (e.g., heterogeneous data modalities, diverse task pipelines). Unlike prior systems that treat LLMs mainly as code generators following rigid workflows, we fully exploit their reasoning capabilities to allow agents to determine their own workflows on the fly. To this end, we propose SE2, a self-exploring and self-evolving paradigm for adaptive edge AI development. Inspired by human learning processes, we distill development into four atomic operations (retrieve, reason, code, test), and empower agents to explore workflows by dynamically and adaptively selecting an appropriate next operation based on the current context. Extensive evaluations show that, with adaptive workflows, the synthesized programs achieve up to 26% higher accuracy and consume 28% lower resources than state-of-the-art baselines, while maintaining superior adaptability to unforeseen edge AI scenarios. This highlights the potential of reasoning-driven adaptive agentic workflow in edge AI development."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "4379dc66d837b5ef0d5f346e2cc05ac9a5be8388", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.38946", "CorpusId": 295373356}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/4379dc66d837b5ef0d5f346e2cc05ac9a5be8388", "title": "MAS-Loop: Beyond One-Shot Orchestration towards Self-Evolving Multi-Agent Systems", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.38946, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-28", "authors": [{"authorId": "2260832033", "name": "He-Hai Lin"}, {"authorId": "2308550856", "name": "Qi Yang"}, {"authorId": "2383968687", "name": "Cheng-Wei Qin"}], "abstract": "Training-time multi-agent system (MAS) design seeks to learn reusable orchestration policies, yet prevailing approaches remain largely one-shot: an Orchestrator generates a system but only observes and learns from its terminal outcome. This paradigm discards execution trajectories that expose system-level failures and contain fine-grained information beyond the terminal outcome, leaving no direct basis for revising the source MAS, evaluating the updated MAS, or learning from the resulting contrast. To this end, we introduce MAS-Loop, an execution-grounded framework that closes this feedback loop by coupling an Orchestrator with a trajectory-conditioned Refiner. For each task, the Orchestrator constructs and executes a source MAS to obtain an outcome score and an intermediate trajectory. Conditioned on the trajectory, the Refiner localizes an actionable failure, formulates a trajectory-grounded diagnosis, and proposes a bounded patch to the agents, information flow, or execution configuration. The executable updated MAS is re-executed on the same task to measure its realized outcome under stochastic execution. This pair-MAS comparison provides complementary learning signals: the Orchestrator learns to generate strong systems with fewer recoverable failures, whereas the Refiner learns to expose and repair observed weaknesses, thereby driving policy-level co-evolution across training iterations. Experiments show that MAS-Loop achieves the strongest overall performance among the evaluated baselines, with consistent gains from refinement across all evaluated benchmarks. The co-evolution process also yields MAS-JudgeCorpus, a dataset of execution-grounded source-updated pairs with orchestration- and trajectory-level supervision, which can provide the community with support for MAS reward modeling and trajectory-aware investigation."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "1e8c6bdae52ce7358f3f9a0f09dab6b708fc95c3", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.38468", "CorpusId": 295373189}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/1e8c6bdae52ce7358f3f9a0f09dab6b708fc95c3", "title": "Cinematize from Unreal: Agentic Choreography for Controllable World Simulation", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.38468, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-28", "authors": [{"authorId": "2228270492", "name": "Zi-Fan Song"}, {"authorId": "2053160406", "name": "Caihua Shan"}, {"authorId": "2371389115", "name": "Kai-Tao Song"}, {"authorId": "2124527879", "name": "Yifan Yang"}, {"authorId": "2269021512", "name": "Dong-Sheng Li"}, {"authorId": "2293449649", "name": "Cai-Rong Zhao"}], "abstract": "Controllable world simulation requires more than visually plausible frames: scenes must retain persistent state, coordinated actions, and editable temporal structure. Generative world models offer flexible visual synthesis, but their outputs do not always expose the objects and transitions needed for precise revision. Graphics engines provide executable state and direct control, yet translating language into valid operations requires specialized knowledge of assets, geometry, and interdependent APIs. We present CineU (Cinematize from Unreal), an agentic system for language-driven world orchestration and cinematic choreography within Unreal Engine. From a code-based world modeling perspective, an Unreal project serves as a persistent, inspectable world representation, while agent-generated code specifies operations whose effects can be executed and verified. CineU combines hierarchical orchestration, cascaded asset retrieval and geometry-constrained placement, capability-aligned code synthesis, and multi-stage verification with localized repair. Shared scene and timeline context coordinates entities, actions, camera trajectories, and shot boundaries while supporting dependency-aware revisions. Experiments on scene construction, selective editing, multi-shot cinematography, and component ablations show how specialized execution knowledge and engine feedback improve task completion and targeted recovery. CineU illustrates how executable world state can complement learned visual generation to support controllable and revisable simulated worlds."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "6b354058e25ab0184a8cf84934bc09faaa8fdde7", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.36660", "CorpusId": 294524268}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6b354058e25ab0184a8cf84934bc09faaa8fdde7", "title": "The View from Nowhere: Population-Centered Agent Evolution", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.36660, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-27", "authors": [{"authorId": "2521792727", "name": "Shuo Yan"}, {"authorId": "2521582669", "name": "Ning-Ning Hou"}, {"authorId": "2326915783", "name": "Hao-Yu He"}, {"authorId": "2374041291", "name": "Hao-Chen Pei"}, {"authorId": "2520331106", "name": "Shang Gao"}], "abstract": "Self-improving AI systems can revise the designs that govern their own behavior. Yet existing approaches center improvement on selected parent agents and their evolutionary experience, leaving the broader population largely underused. We identify a substantial gap between population coverage (i.e., the tasks solved by at least one evolved variant) and the performance of the best individual agent. This gap shows that useful capabilities and diagnostic evidence emerge across the population without being consolidated into its leading agents. These observations motivate the view from nowhere, a principle for agent evolution: an agent should be improved by interpreting its behavior through population-wide patterns of success, failure, and prior self-modification. We instantiate this principle as Population-Centered Evolution (PCE). PCE distils task records and modification histories from the population into a population block, which captures recurring failures, complementary successes, and the effects of previously attempted modifications to guide improvements to a selected parent's harness. Under matched models, initial harnesses, and evolution schedules, PCE achieves 72.1% success on SWE-bench Verified and 96.7% on Polyglot, improving over a strong group-evolution baseline by 3.2 and 5.9 percentage points. In our evaluation, PCE reduces the gap between population coverage and best-agent performance by one-third on SWE-bench and closes it on Polyglot. These results suggest that treating the population as evidence, not merely as candidates for selection, provides a complementary principle for agent evolution."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "f79c920bed65caf56464c91858938b506b35643b", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.37255", "CorpusId": 294621262}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/f79c920bed65caf56464c91858938b506b35643b", "title": "Your Policy Is Secretly a Harness: The Decision Geometry of Agent Interfaces", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.37255, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-27", "authors": [{"authorId": "2356546712", "name": "Jian Mu"}, {"authorId": "2521426933", "name": "Rui-Bo Shen"}, {"authorId": "2382919045", "name": "Yao Shu"}], "abstract": "Language model agents are post-trained and deployed through a harness, the prompts, tools, and control logic that present a task to the model and execute its outputs. Harnesses are often revised and judged by how an already trained model performs under them, which assumes that what the model learned under one harness carries over to the next. We ask what a policy learns from its harness and what survives when the harness changes. Under KL-regularized control, we show that the log odds of an optimal policy recover how strongly its harness favors each action over the others, so a trained policy carries the preferences of its harness. Deployed return then equals the ceiling of the harness, the best return it offers, minus the mismatch of the policy, the return lost to its own suboptimal decisions. Training changes only the mismatch, while changing the harness moves both terms. Comparing harnesses with a fixed model therefore mixes what each harness offers with how well that model uses it. In MiniWoB++, post-training makes a model's preferences specific to its training harness. Reversing the order of the observation fields leaves every optimal decision unchanged yet lowers a trained model's success from 85.0% to 19.2%. Conversely, every new harness that a fixed-model comparison rejects but that raises the ceiling outperforms the original agent after further training under it, and ceiling and mismatch measured beforehand predict these gains. For coding-agent harnesses on Terminal-Bench Lite, ceiling minus mismatch estimated with a stronger model as a stand-in for the optimum reproduces the accuracy ranking, while either term alone does not. Code is available at https://github.com/2020-qqtcg/HRP."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "c113b04a15c1d4057d4b8f2777dd3f4b9e3a4f26", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.34450", "CorpusId": 294367222}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c113b04a15c1d4057d4b8f2777dd3f4b9e3a4f26", "title": "EvolveMAS: A Unified Self-Evolution Framework for Decentralized Multi-Agent Systems", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.34450, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-25", "authors": [{"authorId": "2364789332", "name": "Ya-Lun Wu"}, {"authorId": "2357498174", "name": "Qi-Wei Ma"}, {"authorId": "2372232199", "name": "Bing-Bing Xu"}, {"authorId": "2266277013", "name": "Dan Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2075398318", "name": "Long Bai"}, {"authorId": "2261851935", "name": "Bo-Yang Wang"}, {"authorId": "2259858493", "name": "Jia-Tong Li"}, {"authorId": "2382965039", "name": "Zhe-Qi Lv"}, {"authorId": "1429199889", "name": "Shao-Zu Yuan"}, {"authorId": "2118951553", "name": "Pei-Ying Wang"}, {"authorId": "2506267525", "name": "Jia-Wei Wang"}, {"authorId": "2311074323", "name": "An Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2351809900", "name": "Tat-Seng Chua"}], "abstract": "Decentralized multi-agent systems (MAS) enable large language model agents to interact without centralized control. Agents need to adapt to other agents at test time and reuse accumulated experience to refine their interaction strategies for future interactions across cooperative, mixed-motive, and adversarial settings. However, existing approaches often develop adaptation mechanisms for particular interaction settings. This raises a central question: how can decentralized MAS support test-time adaptation and reliable experience reuse across interaction settings? We propose EvolveMAS, a unified self-evolution framework that supports experience-driven adaptation within and across episodes. EvolveMAS pairs each main agent with a dedicated MemoryAgent for test-time memory governance, which maintains isolated Structured Memory outside the main agent's reasoning context. EvolveMAS organizes adaptation into two complementary processes: Within-Episode Online Adaptation and Cross-Episode Knowledge Evolution. During Within-Episode Online Adaptation, MemoryAgent updates episodic, semantic, and working memory from interaction evidence to inform subsequent actions. During Cross-Episode Knowledge Evolution, the main agent uses memory and episode outcomes to distill reusable interaction knowledge comprising partner profiles and interaction strategies for subsequent episodes. Across five benchmarks and six evaluation configurations spanning the three interaction settings, EvolveMAS achieves the highest normalized average score of 77.2 among the compared methods. Ablations show that test-time memory governance and cross-episode knowledge reuse both improve performance. Interaction traces further illustrate how updated memory and distilled knowledge guide subsequent interactions. These results support experience-driven adaptation in decentralized MAS without updating model parameters."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "886a1998b96df61ee7459d584f766a54482d179d", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.34500", "CorpusId": 294366645}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/886a1998b96df61ee7459d584f766a54482d179d", "title": "CaliMem: Search-Efficient Data Agent via Calibrated Memory", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.34500, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-25", "authors": [{"authorId": "1949334927", "name": "Han-Ze Li"}, {"authorId": "2329081678", "name": "Athan Z. Li"}, {"authorId": "2054842299", "name": "Fei-Yang Kang"}, {"authorId": "2130518057", "name": "Jia-Chen T. Wang"}, {"authorId": "2475498001", "name": "Yu-Han Chen"}, {"authorId": "2357923277", "name": "Rui-Shan Liu"}, {"authorId": "2268321162", "name": "Ruo-Xi Jia"}], "abstract": "Data curation is a first-order design choice in machine learning, yet tailoring training data to a downstream model and task remains costly in labor, expertise, and compute. Datasets are therefore often processed through fixed pipelines and reused across applications. We show that this assumption is fragile: across 55 dataset--model tasks spanning biological domains, the same curation recipe can help one model while hurting another. Effective curation must adapt to the downstream setting, motivating automated search over data recipes. We first build an agentic system that searches for curation policies at deployment, improving data utility via \\textit{test-time curation (TTC)}. Although achieving performance gains, its search is driven largely by recent feedback and remains local. Adding persistent memory helps the agent retain evidence across iterations and reveals a deeper problem: it forms claims that extend beyond the conditions in which they were observed, sometimes abandoning an entire strategy after a single unsuccessful trial. To address this problem, we introduce CaliMem, a calibrated-memory agentic loop that requires the agent to predict the outcome of each proposed policy before executing it. Comparing this prediction with the observed result gives the agent a \\textit{reality check} on the claims behind its proposal. It uses the prediction error to retain supported claims, revise mistaken ones, and specify the conditions under which a conclusion holds. This makes the agent's beliefs testable, reduces overgeneralization, and supports more effective long-horizon exploration. Across biological and LLM post-training tasks, CaliMem consistently outperforms fixed curation baselines and published methods, and exceeds full-data training by up to 19.4\\%. Code is open-sourced."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "24d9e11aba9169ff864082b0a892ac6add32a831", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.35966", "CorpusId": 294524551}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/24d9e11aba9169ff864082b0a892ac6add32a831", "title": "EMMA: Structured Episodic Memory for Multi-Agent Workflow Synthesis", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.35966, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-25", "authors": [{"authorId": "2288267095", "name": "Rui-Jia Niu"}, {"authorId": "2449597062", "name": "Kai-Wen Liu"}, {"authorId": "2253245279", "name": "Rose Yu"}, {"authorId": "2873011", "name": "K. Klinkner"}], "abstract": "Deploying LLM agents in specialized domains often require fine-grained task-specific procedures and knowledge a general model lacks, yet parameter training is costly and non-interpretable, and hand-engineered agent systems shift the cost to expert labor. Episodic memory updates at context level, but single agent adaptation struggles to alleviate cross-task interference, especially with complex tasks such as multi-application navigation or data pool analysis. Although multi-agent evolution alleviates this problem, existing methods lack clear definitions on update rules and rely on the backbone model to decide where and how much the system changes. This ill-scoped evolution makes learning follow the backbone's structural preferences rather than the observed failures: one backbone accumulates skills while another spawns agents. We present EMMA (structured Episodic Memory for Multi-Agent workflow synthesis), which treats the configuration of a multi-agent system (controller routing memory, specialists inheriting a generalist prompt, and per-step procedural skills) as episodic memory. EMMA makes the ownership and admissibility of updates explicit: a reflector assigns each diagnosed failure to the scope owning its correction and proposes one edit within it, and backbone-independent guards keep every accepted edit scoped, incremental and bounded. Across KramaBench, AppWorld and BBEH with five backbones and four baselines (ACE, GEPA, EvoMAS and ADAS), EMMA attains the best overall performance on every benchmark, up to 32.5% over the strongest baseline, maintains consistent improvements across backbones, and uses 17-38% fewer training executions than the most efficient baseline."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "8d0b14bf320e12c5afe83ca847a19b54d61b5fb2", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.33375", "CorpusId": 294228335}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/8d0b14bf320e12c5afe83ca847a19b54d61b5fb2", "title": "AgentOrch: How Close Can Global Orchestration Come to Step-by-Step Interaction?", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.33375, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-24", "authors": [{"authorId": "2342746625", "name": "Zi-Jie Liu"}, {"authorId": "2007676791", "name": "Qi-Yang Huang"}, {"authorId": "2004228925", "name": "Jinhao Duan"}, {"authorId": "2521971730", "name": "Yueh-Che Lee"}, {"authorId": "47800700", "name": "T. Joseph"}, {"authorId": "2500521890", "name": "Zayne L. Turner"}, {"authorId": "2520169604", "name": "Adam Seligman"}, {"authorId": "2284728259", "name": "Tian-Long Chen"}, {"authorId": "2276485344", "name": "Zhao-Zhuo Xu"}], "abstract": "Enterprise workflow agents typically rely on step-by-step interaction (Step-by-Step): the agent executes an action, observes its result, and invokes a large language model (LLM) again to decide the next step. Global orchestration (Global) instead generates a complete executable workflow in advance and executes it without further LLM calls, substantially reducing inference overhead, but it remains less accurate than Step-by-Step. We study how close Global can come to Step-by-Step and what narrows the remaining gap. We introduce AgentOrch, a benchmark that directly compares global orchestration with step-by-step interaction on 650 enterprise SOPs and 3,220 runtime variants under matched tools, environments, and evaluation. Step-by-Step remains more accurate for every evaluated model, but the gap has shrunk by about 79% from early models to the strongest recent model. We identify three routes to further narrowing this gap. Incremental workflow construction improves Global accuracy by an average of 9.98 percentage points (pp) without runtime execution feedback. Revising complete workflows with execution feedback improves all evaluated models, including 6.13 pp on held-out runtime variants. Targeted training on complete workflows improves accuracy by an average of 12.18 pp across all five trained models. Although a gap remains, global orchestration offers a promising path toward enterprise agents that deliver higher accuracy with fewer LLM calls."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "40008e6b7d8a029dd1b67ba09d7369ddd386d26f", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.33539", "CorpusId": 294228277}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/40008e6b7d8a029dd1b67ba09d7369ddd386d26f", "title": "CoEvolve: Counterfactual-Guided Co-Evolution of Memory and Collaboration in Multi-Agent Systems", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.33539, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-24", "authors": [{"authorId": "2521774953", "name": "Xu-Fei Shi"}, {"authorId": "2187417059", "name": "De-Rong Xu"}, {"authorId": "2260766940", "name": "Wei Chen"}, {"authorId": "2445224571", "name": "Yan-Yu Yao"}, {"authorId": "2227734260", "name": "Zhi Zheng"}, {"authorId": "2320264786", "name": "Tong Xu"}], "abstract": "Large language model (LLM)-based multi-agent systems have demonstrated remarkable capabilities across diverse tasks. Yet their memory and collaboration architectures often remain static, constraining continued adaptation and improvement over time. Recent work addresses this limitation by evolving memory or collaboration based on interaction experience. However, updates to these interdependent components are not necessarily aligned: an evolved workflow may require experience that memory does not provide, while evolved memory may encode guidance that the workflow cannot use. To address this problem, we propose CoEvolve, a skill-centered framework for aligned memory--collaboration evolution. CoEvolve represents reusable local collaboration patterns as skills that link evolving collaboration decisions to their supporting memories. It further compares original and counterfactual trajectories to identify local evolution signals, which coordinate updates to the skill library, skill memory, and executor memories across rounds. Experiments on the ALFWorld and WebShop benchmarks confirm that CoEvolve achieves the best overall performance among the evaluated baselines, while ablation studies further validate the effectiveness of aligned co-evolution and counterfactual-guided evolution."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "fd2e2febeb36f24308e9104762991713c067fcb9", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.32686", "CorpusId": 293940377}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/fd2e2febeb36f24308e9104762991713c067fcb9", "title": "OpenDraco: A Platform for Engineering and Experimenting with Configurable Multi-Agent Architectures for Software Engineering", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32686, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-23", "authors": [{"authorId": "2517972188", "name": "Xiang-Feng-Ye Pan"}, {"authorId": "2360546525", "name": "Vincenzo De Martino"}, {"authorId": "2214087158", "name": "Matias Martinez"}], "abstract": "Experimenting with LLM-based multi-agent systems (MAS) involves an iterative cycle of modeling configurations, executing and evaluating them, comparing results, and refining system designs. Existing approaches provide limited end-to-end support for this experimentation lifecycle. We present OpenDraco, an open-source platform that integrates these activities into a single workflow. OpenDraco represents agents, prompts, tools, models, parameters, and communication topology in exportable, versioned JSON specifications, translated into executable LangGraph workflows supporting different interaction structures. Users can modify and re-evaluate configurations using local or cloud-based LLMs. We instantiate OpenDraco for automated program repair (APR) and evaluate a sequential baseline and seven reconstructed configurations on synthetic and real-world SWE-bench Lite issues. The results reveal substantial cost differences among configurations with similar repair outcomes, while the baseline resolves 12 of 77 SWE-bench issues using open-source LLMs. Additional proof-of-concept tasks illustrate applicability beyond APR. OpenDraco supports iterative experimentation with heterogeneous MAS configurations, enabling researchers to examine their outcomes and costs within a common environment. Demonstration video: https://youtu.be/278sOR4lBEk."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "06cababf3ab1ce5ed336f106edf7cb9c7fc011b9", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.32483", "CorpusId": 293940791}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/06cababf3ab1ce5ed336f106edf7cb9c7fc011b9", "title": "When Agents Fail: Model or Harness--Who's to Blame?", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.32483, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-23", "authors": [{"authorId": "2116577679", "name": "Shao Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2376371350", "name": "Hang-Fan Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2308038006", "name": "Yuan-Yuan Shi"}, {"authorId": "2363724901", "name": "Hao Li"}, {"authorId": "2473419793", "name": "Min-Le Su"}, {"authorId": "2344931909", "name": "Kang-Cong Li"}, {"authorId": "2517990076", "name": "Jing-Ping Wang"}, {"authorId": "145468830", "name": "Chun-Jiang Mu"}, {"authorId": "2439283810", "name": "C. Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2378851357", "name": "Yang Chen"}, {"authorId": "2421192951", "name": "Bo Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2362971451", "name": "Shu-Yue Hu"}], "abstract": "LLM agents combine models with execution harnesses, so agentic benchmark performance reflects their joint behavior. We argue that harnesses should therefore be evaluated through their interactions with underlying models. We develop a framework combining five complementary measures: (i) Valid Completion Rate (VCR), how reliably agents finish normally and deliver evaluable results, irrespective of task correctness; (ii) Model-Harness Fit, how well a harness supports task achievement through valid completion for a given model; (iii) Cross-Model Fit, a harness's overall suitability across evaluated models; (iv) Interaction Residuals, pairing-specific execution strengths and weaknesses hidden by component-level summaries; and (v) Token Load, the token demands of agent execution, including failed attempts. We evaluate four general-purpose harnesses (OpenCode, Codex CLI, Pi CLI, and DeepAgents) with four core models, plus two fixed native model-harness systems, on six benchmarks covering context management, tool use, long-context planning, multimodal interaction, and artifact handoff. Including native references and stress conditions, 111 evaluation settings yield 52,168 trajectories. Harness rankings vary across models and benchmarks; task performance and valid completion can favor different pairings, and similar task scores can accompany distinct execution profiles. OpenCode ranks first in cross-model performance, while DeepSeek V4 Flash Preview maintains consistently high Mean VCR across harnesses on the shared four-benchmark panel. These findings support evaluating model-harness interactions, training transferable harness-use capabilities in models, and harness designs adapted to the model and task."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "2b6a8796c41c2be528454dce0e564e2b7c4acbb0", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.31436", "CorpusId": 293894418}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/2b6a8796c41c2be528454dce0e564e2b7c4acbb0", "title": "Conservative Meta-Agent Search: Architecture Adaptation under Non-Stationarity", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.31436, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-22", "authors": [{"authorId": "2385463345", "name": "A. Alzahrani"}], "abstract": "Automated agentic-system design is typically evaluated on static task suites with immediate scalar rewards. Deployed systems face persistent shift, delayed dependent feedback, correlated component failures, and costly architecture changes. We formalize meta-agent architecture search as sequential incumbent replacement with switching costs and hard risk constraints and introduce Conservative Meta-Agent Search (CMAS). CMAS retrieves archived architectures and proposes typed graph edits, then scores each against the incumbent by paired shadow replay under common random numbers, with interventional component credit. Promotion requires an empirically calibrated, variance-penalized simultaneous score above a pre-specified margin, and is followed by a simulated canary stage and pre-specified rollback. Since backtests cannot certify meta-level skill, we introduce MetaInvest-Bench, a regime-switching simulator whose specified worker competence makes a surrogate-optimal architecture computable, with leakage-controlled replay, adversarial governance tracks, and five negative controls. Financial streams serve as a risk-sensitive testbed for meta-level generalization; we claim no alpha. Across 96 streams, CMAS cuts normalized mean surrogate dynamic-oracle regret from 0.116 to 0.104 and raises held-out utility from 0.44 to 0.48 over the strongest adaptive baseline; when extra workers add no value, it contracts to near-single-agent teams. Its deployment inference cost is ~6% lower, though its search-inclusive token cost is the highest of the methods compared. The observed per-update false-promotion rate is 3.4% against a nominal 5% target, and 4.8% in a sealed rerun. The calibration is empirical: it concerns an update-level replay statistic on an author-designed benchmark, and guarantees neither full-utility improvement, nor absence of simulated canary-stage harm, nor sequence-level error control."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "d1282dbd5a8137c8fcb1a08fa626a77c2940b284", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.31062", "CorpusId": 293894765}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d1282dbd5a8137c8fcb1a08fa626a77c2940b284", "title": "SCOPE: Compiling Natural-Language Policies into Neurosymbolic Predicate Programs", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.31062, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-22", "authors": [{"authorId": "2340040385", "name": "A. Mazza"}, {"authorId": "34490455", "name": "Elad Levi"}], "abstract": "Policy enforcement for LLM agents has shifted from single-turn content moderation to compound, application-specific policies evaluated over multi-turn transcripts and tool-call trajectories. Fine-tuned guardrail models are efficient but degrade on compound policies, and while LLM-as-a-judge approaches achieve higher accuracy, they incur high inference cost and tend to produce unstable predictions. Neurosymbolic methods offer a natural alternative, decomposing the policy into narrow neural decisions composed under explicit logic. However, existing symbolic layers lack the required expressiveness and are optimized against fixed data rather than the policy itself. To address this, we introduce SCOPE, which compiles a policy into an executable program of neural predicates executed by small models, deterministic extractors, and code that composes their outputs. The program is optimized jointly with its training data, using adversarial generation of counterexamples that target where the program deviates from the policy. We evaluate SCOPE on a benchmark of real-world policy tasks against prompt optimization, task decomposition, and workflow generation methods, achieving the highest average accuracy across tasks and models. Programs optimized with the weakest executor transfer to other models without further optimization. This allows them to be deployed in production on inexpensive models, where they reach accuracy comparable to frontier reasoning models. Unlike these monolithic models, SCOPE produces substantially more stable predictions, and each verdict is interpretable through the predicate outputs that produced it. Additionally, we validate the key components of our method through an ablation study. SCOPE thus enables reliable, interpretable, and efficient policy enforcement at production scale."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "ae04a4539d0b3a9274e8115829a1cb47e06d0b39", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.31709", "CorpusId": 293936006}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/ae04a4539d0b3a9274e8115829a1cb47e06d0b39", "title": "Mining the Design Space of Multi-Agent Workflows", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.31709, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-22", "authors": [{"authorId": "83579257", "name": "Xavier F. Cadet"}, {"authorId": "2313693827", "name": "Jun-Yan Cheng"}, {"authorId": "2327213146", "name": "Peter Chin"}, {"authorId": "46666605", "name": "Kyle Richardson"}], "abstract": "Designing large language model (LLM) multi-agent systems (MAS) still relies on trial and error and informal\"folk wisdom.\"Manual studies test only a few communication structures or topologies (e.g., sequential, parallel, shared-memory, or debate), while automated optimizers discard search trajectories after selecting a task-specific workflow. We present a two-phase pipeline for mining reproducible design principles from workflow code and execution traces. We repurpose AFlow's MCTS optimizer across six composable operators and 26 deterministically verified Enigmata tasks, retaining all intermediate rounds to build a corpus of 28,568 executable workflows. A sandboxed AutoResearch loop then mines orchestration code and prompt templates under an $L_0$-penalized validation objective and tests candidate principles on held-out searches. Across 300 feature-mining trials, we identify effects that persist on held-out searches across three levels of workflow design: workflows beginning with independent solvers and one-shot aggregation (Star-first) perform worse ($\\Delta = -0.045$); persistent shared documents (SharedMemory) outperform debate ($\\Delta = +0.135$); and prompt specificity and placeholder-based context routing yield gains comparable to communication-structure selection ($\\Delta = +0.118$). More broadly, our pipeline and results show the feasibility of turning discarded search trajectories into a statistical testbed for discovering and validating multi-agent workflow design principles."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "c513d51f19a43f8d766413fdea60bf787d1b3e5e", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.29276", "CorpusId": 293844961}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c513d51f19a43f8d766413fdea60bf787d1b3e5e", "title": "Design and Tuning of Multi-Agent Systems Using Bayesian Evolutionary Optimization", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.29276, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-21", "authors": [{"authorId": "2501497044", "name": "Dominik Jehle"}, {"authorId": "2395769191", "name": "Tom Zehle"}], "abstract": "Designing effective multi-agent systems (MAS) remains a challenging and resource-intensive task. We propose a Bayesian Evolutionary Optimization framework for automatically discovering task-specific MAS topologies. The approach combines evolutionary operators to generate diverse workflow graphs with Bayesian optimization to focus cost intensive evaluations on promising candidates. In contrast to LLM-based candidate selection, our approach uses a graph-aware Gaussian Process and Expected Improvement to provide an explicit selection criterion during evolutionary search. We evaluate the method on BigCodeBench for code generation and on a tabular preprocessing benchmark using TabPFN. On BigCodeBench, evolved architectures achieve the highest overall performance among the evaluated agent systems. On temporal tabular datasets, optimized MAS preprocessing improves over the default TabPFN model on several tasks, while remaining competitive on IID datasets. Overall, the results demonstrate the potential of Bayesian selection for guiding evolutionary MAS search under expensive evaluation budgets."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "ef12b85b1632958199fc5fc10f5445787cf5c375", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.30042", "CorpusId": 293856715}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/ef12b85b1632958199fc5fc10f5445787cf5c375", "title": "HGM-Dual: Lineage-Aware Guided Agent Harness Optimization for Long-Horizon Planning", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30042, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-21", "authors": [{"authorId": "2277299467", "name": "Sudipta Paul"}, {"authorId": "2003336037", "name": "Peng-Rui Quan"}, {"authorId": "2344642721", "name": "Aounon Kumar"}, {"authorId": "2261733814", "name": "Nicholas Tzou"}, {"authorId": "2283136782", "name": "Vivek Kulkarni"}, {"authorId": "1667831197", "name": "Srinivas Chappidi"}], "abstract": "Harness optimization, which improves an agent's prompts, tools, and workflows, is an increasingly effective way to enhance agents built on frozen or closed-source LLMs. Leading approaches such as the Darwin--Godel Machine and the Huxley--Godel Machine (HGM) rely on recursive self-improvement and were developed mainly for coding, where the competence needed to improve the agent coincides with the competence being improved. Planning tasks, in which an agent must produce a sequence of tool calls that jointly satisfies interacting constraints, break this alignment. There, open-ended self-modification repeatedly explores ineffective changes and consumes substantial evaluation budgets. We introduce HGM-Dual, a diagnostic-guided framework for budget-efficient harness evolution for planning agents. First, HGM-Dual represents agent evolution as lineage-aware context. This context records what each ancestor changed, which behaviors improved or regressed, and which modifications were ineffective, giving the editor explicit edit-outcome attribution instead of an unstructured archive of past agents. Second, HGM-Dual introduces error-conditioned dual-path child generation. For each selected parent, it generates a general child that preserves open-ended exploration and specialized children that target the harness components most likely responsible for the parent's highest-ranked constraint violations. HGM-Dual outperforms the strongest baseline by up to 14.45% relative improvement in case accuracy while matching HGM variants'final performance with 2x-4x fewer evaluation budget."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "c6532750d7744506ece01c8d7daae9424b905e13", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.30499", "CorpusId": 293894326}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c6532750d7744506ece01c8d7daae9424b905e13", "title": "Can LLMs Improve Their Own Harness? A Controlled Study on ARC-AGI-3", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.30499, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-21", "authors": [{"authorId": "2291593072", "name": "Jens Lehmann"}, {"authorId": "2327466746", "name": "Sahar Vahdati"}], "abstract": "A generally intelligent system acquires new skills efficiently without requiring humans to redesign its software scaffold for every new task. Yet language models rarely act alone. A harness is a symbolic program that orchestrates calls to the model, handling perception, memory, tool use, recovery, and inference allocation on the model's behalf. ARC-AGI-3 is an interactive benchmark in which agents must explore unfamiliar environments, figure out their goals on the fly, and keep learning as they go. On it, human-steered systems already report ceiling scores across the public games, so we know effective designs exist. We ask whether frontier models can build and improve such a harness on their own. Through a fixed coding bootstrap, each model constructs a complete task harness, acts through that harness on five development games, and revises the entire codebase twice. Every version is evaluated on twenty games that never influence adaptation. We measure task performance with Relative Human Action Efficiency (RHAE), which combines level completion with action efficiency relative to human baselines, and record dollar-denominated inference cost as a practical resource measure. Across 27 lineages, harness performance ranges from zero to near-ceiling scores. Two revisions raise mean held-out RHAE by only 2.80 points (95% interval [-1.44,7.35]). Development gains overstate held-out improvement, and reductions in inference cost can come at the expense of task performance. Human-steered systems demonstrate further headroom. These findings reveal a gap between autonomous harness construction and reliable adaptation under this two-revision protocol: even strong task performance does not ensure that subsequent revisions generalize or preserve existing capabilities."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "bbaf552602d4c1b280790faa242e4616d5505590", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.27531", "CorpusId": 293809074}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/bbaf552602d4c1b280790faa242e4616d5505590", "title": "Automated Multi-Agent Organization Design: From Architecture Search to Organizational Learning", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27531, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-20", "authors": [{"authorId": "2354377707", "name": "Shao-Jun Xia"}, {"authorId": "2350419267", "name": "Yun-Er Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2307032631", "name": "Hui-Xin Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2500267873", "name": "Di Wang"}, {"authorId": "2492070232", "name": "Yi-Fan Jiang"}, {"authorId": "2500961647", "name": "Liang Liu"}], "abstract": "Agentic system design is increasingly shifting from hand-crafted prompts and workflows toward automated search over agentic programs and interaction structures, with multi-agent systems receiving growing attention. Yet a central question remains unresolved: when does an automatically generated multi-agent structure exhibit a functionally meaningful division of responsibility? We argue that automated multi-agent design should move beyond searching for effective architectures toward identifying which responsibilities are functionally necessary, how they should be allocated, and when they should change. To explore this, we propose Automated Multi-Agent Organization Design (AMOD), which treats organization itself as a first-class design object, explicitly modeling how agent teams are composed, what functional responsibilities the agents assume, how they coordinate, and how collective decisions are made. We formulate AMOD as an execution-driven design loop. For each task, an automated designer instantiates an organization by assigning functional responsibilities and coordination rules to a set of agents. After execution, task outcomes, intermediate artifacts, and interaction traces are examined to identify which responsibilities may be missing, duplicated, or ineffective, and the organization is revised accordingly. We also propose testing discovered responsibilities by removing them, replacing them with generic roles, or changing their assignment under matched resource budgets, with complementary evaluations. AMOD provides a principled foundation for evidence-based and adaptive multi-agent organization design."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "eb7dc59898e4ee717481e6ef21c155eb83a68639", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.27495", "CorpusId": 293809476}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/eb7dc59898e4ee717481e6ef21c155eb83a68639", "title": "Self-Evolving Agents through Privileged Retrospective Distillation", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27495, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-20", "authors": [{"authorId": "2387206689", "name": "Yu-Hang Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2328023239", "name": "Yi-Fan Wu"}, {"authorId": "2405641662", "name": "Bo Peng"}, {"authorId": "2330281890", "name": "Ming-Yi Wang"}, {"authorId": "2286338976", "name": "Jia-Yi Liu"}, {"authorId": "2323788930", "name": "Xiang-Jun Fan"}, {"authorId": "2340399373", "name": "Li-Zhu Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2352021017", "name": "Zhuo-Kai Zhao"}], "abstract": "Self-evolving agents aim to improve from their own experience rather than remain fixed after deployment, but naturally collected trajectories often lack ground-truth solutions or verifier-derived rewards. We introduce Retrospective Self-Distillation (RSD), a multi-round framework that turns such experience into dense supervision using privileged retrospective context. For each on-policy trajectory, the same underlying model acts as both student and teacher: the teacher re-scores the student's realized actions while conditioning on cross-task precedents and lessons, together with turn-level reflection and future hindsight from the completed trajectory. The resulting teacher distribution is distilled back into the policy through on-policy self-distillation. Across rounds, the experience bank grows and the retrospective context is refreshed, allowing the model, its data, and its supervision to co-evolve. We further introduce frontier-guided context-policy evolution, where a stronger frontier model proposes constrained edits to how privileged context is constructed. Across ALFWorld, ScienceWorld, and SearchQA with Qwen3-8B and Qwen3-14B, RSD improves performance by up to 59.7% relative to the base model, while frontier-guided context-policy evolution adds up to a further 44.7% relative improvement over a fixed context policy. These results show that naturally accumulated experience can be converted into persistent model capability without ground-truth trajectories or verifier-derived rewards for the core policy update."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "4529a4eb30cf72b9f37f1e96fcdaa2d7ed123238", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.27435", "CorpusId": 293809157}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/4529a4eb30cf72b9f37f1e96fcdaa2d7ed123238", "title": "Harness Self-Evolution for Long-Horizon Terminal Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.27435, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-20", "authors": [{"authorId": "2328617416", "name": "Ru-Han Wang"}, {"authorId": "2249001715", "name": "Yu-Cheng Shi"}, {"authorId": "2375157272", "name": "Zong-Xia Li"}, {"authorId": "2403718571", "name": "Jun-Yao Yang"}, {"authorId": "2268636229", "name": "Zhong-Zhi Li"}, {"authorId": "2447743497", "name": "Yue Yu"}, {"authorId": "2238955130", "name": "Hai-Tao Mi"}, {"authorId": "2292161506", "name": "Dong-Ruo Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2422670781", "name": "Leoweiliang"}], "abstract": "Long-horizon terminal agents may solve a task in one run yet fail across repeated runs. Stochastic errors can propagate through actions and context, creating a gap between capability and reliable execution. We present RiseHarness, a framework for iteratively improving agent harnesses with model weights fixed. It focuses on flipping tasks with both successful and failed runs under the same configuration, using successful runs as references for failure analysis. RiseHarness combines agent trajectories with internal harness traces from a runtime monitor to examine how model actions interact with command execution, feedback, context management, and completion decisions. A Harness Handbook links this evidence to harness behaviors and source code, helping identify recurring problems in mechanisms shared across tasks. RiseHarness then searches other harness frameworks for relevant implementations and adapts them as design references to the current model and execution protocol. Candidate changes undergo behavioral checks and repeated task evaluation; versions with measured gains seed the next iteration. This loop integrates suitable mechanisms from different frameworks into a single harness and uses new execution evidence to guide further changes. We evaluate task success and reliability across models, ablate harness execution evidence and external research, and test transfer to unseen benchmarks with the same model. RiseHarness treats a specific model as the unit of harness adaptation, studying how changes to shared interaction mechanisms can make existing capabilities more reliable across tasks."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "c55aa369ac67549f7b4b13e3b405886a5d94da62", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.28785", "CorpusId": 293845201}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c55aa369ac67549f7b4b13e3b405886a5d94da62", "title": "Measure First, Then Allocate: Calibrated Rollout Budgets for MCP Agents in Reinforcement Learning", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.28785, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-20", "authors": [{"authorId": "2263121822", "name": "Hieu Tran"}, {"authorId": "1576489304", "name": "Zonghai Yao"}, {"authorId": "2335844854", "name": "Sunjae Kwon"}, {"authorId": "2261455807", "name": "Hong Yu"}], "abstract": "Training tool-using language models requires costly interaction, yet identical rewards within a rollout group provide no group-relative learning signal. We use reward disagreement to guide harness evaluation and the allocation of rollouts for policy training in Model Context Protocol (MCP) environments. We first introduce Discord, which tests harness edits using exact paired inference and decision-preserving early stopping: only disagreeing paired outcomes favor one harness over another. This insight motivates prospective allocation in BRACE (Bank-calibrated Rollout Allocation under Correlated Episodes). BRACE combines historical task evidence, uncertainty in task success rates, and a correlated reward model to forecast mixed-reward groups before allocating generation budget. Controlled harness experiments favor Discord, while live MCP experiments expose a retention-cost trade-off. At three Qwen3-VL model scales, BRACE exceeds the strongest plotted baseline peaks with fewer new training rollouts; historical-bank collection is counted separately. It also leads the compared methods on BFCL and NESTFUL without further training. Ablations probe the bank, estimator, and allocation score; prospective evaluation checks forecast calibration. These results support forecasting reward contrast before generation."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "f673633a1995628fe28926c14b82951efb071654", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.26861", "CorpusId": 293804317}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/f673633a1995628fe28926c14b82951efb071654", "title": "Can It Actually Work? Synthesizing Functional CAD Mechanisms", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.26861, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-19", "authors": [{"authorId": "2510649055", "name": "Wen-Qian Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2275566547", "name": "Yang-Yi Huang"}, {"authorId": "2515993250", "name": "Zi-Geng Xu"}, {"authorId": "2109218779", "name": "Wei-Yu Liu"}, {"authorId": "2334589701", "name": "Zhen Liu"}], "abstract": "While LLM agents can generate visually plausible CAD assemblies, these designs often fail to fulfill their intended physical functions. We study functional CAD design, a setting where agents interpret underspecified requests to construct and iteratively revise mechanisms that satisfy functional requirements in physical simulation. Verifying these requirements is nontrivial: different candidate mechanisms may realize the same function through entirely different components, controls, and operating sequences. To bridge this gap, we propose FunCAD-Harness, a framework that translates abstract requirements into candidate-specific physical tests and uses simulation feedback to guide design revisions. To systematically evaluate this capability, we introduce FunCADBench, a benchmark spanning 46 mechanism families with interdependent and multi-step operations. Experiments across frontier LLM agents show that FunCAD-Harness exposes subtle mechanical failures invisible to visual inspection, substantially improving functional success rates over standard simulation-augmented baselines. Ultimately, our work marks a step toward CAD agents capable of translating human intent into working mechanisms."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "8d29a8e3ba3ea7b9d7b8fff154524535168f6a29", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.26770", "CorpusId": 293807587}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/8d29a8e3ba3ea7b9d7b8fff154524535168f6a29", "title": "HarnessPro: Recursive Harness Self-Improvement with Pareto Optimization for Performance-Cost Trade-offs", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.26770, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-19", "authors": [{"authorId": "2352886718", "name": "Zhuo-Shi Pan"}, {"authorId": "2406050612", "name": "Zhi-Yuan Feng"}, {"authorId": "83546711", "name": "Yukuo Cen"}, {"authorId": "2325915210", "name": "Hong-Lin Lin"}, {"authorId": "2284699608", "name": "Jun-Ru Lu"}, {"authorId": "2510533300", "name": "Zhong-Xia Chen"}, {"authorId": "2402763425", "name": "Shuo Yang"}, {"authorId": "2265523027", "name": "H. V. Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2375864860", "name": "Jie Tang"}], "abstract": "Harness development plays a crucial role in building capable AI agents. Recent approaches improve harnesses through recursive self-improvement (RSI), but their performance gains often come with higher execution costs. We introduce HarnessPro, a label-free method for evolving harnesses to improve task performance while reducing execution cost. In this setting, candidate harnesses are evaluated using LLM-generated tests without access to ground-truth solutions or official tests. Specialized agents diagnose execution trajectories, plan updates with different performance-cost priorities, and implement them as candidate harnesses. Rather than returning a single harness, HarnessPro uses normalized weighted squared-distance selection to provide cost-focused (lite), balanced (medium), and performance-focused (heavy) harnesses for different deployment budgets. Selected harnesses enter the next iteration, where execution feedback guides further updates. Our experiments cover Terminal-Bench 2, SWE-Bench Pro, and ProgramBench, using GPT-5.5 with Codex and GLM-5.2 with Claude Code. Across these benchmarks using GPT-5.5 with Codex, the heavy mode of HarnessPro achieves an average pass-rate gain of 2.0 percentage points and an average per-task cost reduction of 26.1% relative to RHO. This advantage also holds when using GLM-5.2 with Claude Code. Code is available at https://github.com/pzs19/HarnessPro"}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "7cf8f9618cb7e3d158965c0b29a17fee279938e9", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.25089", "CorpusId": 293807445}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/7cf8f9618cb7e3d158965c0b29a17fee279938e9", "title": "DAGLoop: Aligning Method Design with Experimental Feedback in Automated AI Research", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.25089, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-18", "authors": [{"authorId": "2470954479", "name": "Dengyuxing Wang"}, {"authorId": "2360840966", "name": "Yong-Gang Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2332787825", "name": "Qi Cai"}, {"authorId": "2438881144", "name": "Hua-Jiang Zheng"}, {"authorId": "2355729616", "name": "Jun Song"}, {"authorId": "2371159496", "name": "Yang-Qiu Song"}, {"authorId": "2403462291", "name": "Yi-Ke Guo"}], "abstract": "Automated AI research agents aim to improve machine learning methods by iteratively proposing, implementing, and evaluating candidate methods. Existing work uses the outcome of each evaluation to determine how the next candidate should be revised for performance improvement. However, a candidate often combines multiple design decisions, and its overall outcome alone does not reveal which decisions produced their expected effects, whether their required conditions were satisfied, or which decisions should be retained or revised. Inspired by ablation studies in the machine learning community, we propose DAGLoop to test the expected effect of a design decision by comparing implementations with and without that decision while preserving the other decisions it requires. Specifically, DAGLoop translates each design decision's rationale into a testable expectation and pairs it with a targeted experiment, forming an experimental specification for assessing the expected effect under its required conditions. Using these specifications, we formulate ablation planning as the construction of a directed acyclic graph (DAG) from the candidate method's design: nodes associate decisions with tests, and edges encode dependencies. The DAG specifies which decisions each experiment must retain and supports joint tests when an expected effect requires several decisions to act together. Outcomes are recorded with the tested decisions, expectations, and conditions to form structured research memory. This memory guides the agent in retaining experimentally supported decisions, revising unsupported ones, and planning further experiments when the evidence is unresolved. Experiments on dozens of research tasks demonstrate substantial performance improvements achieved by DAGLoop across diverse executable research settings. Code and evaluation scripts are available at https://github.com/deng34567/DAGLoop."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "dade126e1300171226187278f1b9aa4ea8e9ecea", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.24380", "CorpusId": 293686538}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/dade126e1300171226187278f1b9aa4ea8e9ecea", "title": "Train Locally, Learn Together: Distributed Multi-Agent Training via ADMM", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.24380, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-17", "authors": [{"authorId": "2350502165", "name": "Yi-Wei Chen"}, {"authorId": "2355718070", "name": "Chang-Sheng Wang"}, {"authorId": "2383170439", "name": "Yi-Cheng Lang"}, {"authorId": "2319604827", "name": "Qing Qu"}, {"authorId": "2311117948", "name": "Alfred O. Hero"}, {"authorId": "2332923951", "name": "Si-Jia Liu"}], "abstract": "Multi-agent systems (MAS) can outperform individual language-model agents by combining specialized roles, diverse reasoning processes, and collaborative decision-making. However, collaborative MAS training is typically centralized, requiring a common trainer to aggregate role-local trajectories and learning signals and maintain model and optimization states. This motivates a distributed multi-agent learning paradigm in which agents improve system-level capabilities through role-local learning and decentralized communication, paving the way toward scalable, resilient, and self-organizing MAS. To this end, we introduce, to the best of our knowledge, the first optimization-grounded framework for distributed MAS training based on consensus alternating direction method of multipliers (ADMM). We formulate collaborative MAS learning as a consensus-constrained finite-sum optimization problem, naturally lending itself to distributed optimization via ADMM. The proposed approach alternates role-local learning with inter-agent consensus using distributed communication protocols such as ring all-reduce, enabling joint optimization while keeping each agent's data and training state local. We evaluate our framework on distributed safety alignment and general reasoning tasks. Under compromised-agent settings, ADMM can reduce HarmBench attack success rate (ASR) by up to 26.4%, while achieving reasoning gains comparable to centralized training. These results show that distributed MAS training retains the benefits of centralized joint optimization without requiring a centralized trainer."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "d2f8a315f1963a86f70bdd0af1b407344ad16f5d", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.24537", "CorpusId": 293685745}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d2f8a315f1963a86f70bdd0af1b407344ad16f5d", "title": "Write Once, Query Many: Evolving Compact Reusable Memory for Long Videos", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.24537, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-17", "authors": [{"authorId": "2342774450", "name": "Ke-Rui Chen"}, {"authorId": "2364801583", "name": "Jing-Lu Wang"}, {"authorId": "2515591883", "name": "Yun Wang"}, {"authorId": "2262278343", "name": "Xin-Jiang Wang"}, {"authorId": "2341904904", "name": "Yan Lu"}], "abstract": "Long videos often exceed the context capacity of multimodal large language models (MLLMs), while agentic search repeats costly video processing and grounding for each query. Reusable memory can amortize this cost, but must preserve information for questions unknown during construction. We propose Video-EMem, an evolvable video memory harness built on time-anchored perceptual observations. This representation combines compact free-form text with sparse images to preserve semantic content and complementary visual details across temporal scales, supporting temporal navigation without a fixed semantic schema. A Writer encodes each video once by consolidating overlapping multiscale observations, and a Reader retrieves and organizes evidence solely from the resulting memory. With the underlying models frozen, we evolve their workflows and operators using distinct feedback: query-independent source fidelity and coverage for the Writer, and QA failures in evidence localization and utilization for the Reader. Experiments support this separation for generalization beyond development queries, with our evolution strategy improving average accuracy by 7.2% over the unevolved harness. While reducing storage by about 99.86% on average relative to raw videos, Video-EMem improves Qwen3-VL-30B by an average of 2.6% across three long-video QA benchmarks over direct input of 2,048 frames. Using the same o3 answer model as the state-of-the-art agentic method, it improves average accuracy by 2.5% with lower per-query latency."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "9e495305471dae3123bd3bd6087124f426926457", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.24383", "CorpusId": 293687528}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/9e495305471dae3123bd3bd6087124f426926457", "title": "IF-MAP: Influence-Function-Inspired Agent Prioritization in Multi-Agent Systems", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.24383, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-17", "authors": [{"authorId": "2449900602", "name": "Jie Mao"}, {"authorId": "2436752563", "name": "Ping-Chen Lu"}, {"authorId": "2367194458", "name": "Zi-Kun Qu"}, {"authorId": "2343681730", "name": "Jia-Hang Sun"}, {"authorId": "2302969335", "name": "Xiang-Yi Wang"}, {"authorId": "2382918145", "name": "Zhi-Wei Shang"}, {"authorId": "2343671159", "name": "Zhi-Yong Wang"}, {"authorId": "2284763655", "name": "Guo-Jun Xiong"}, {"authorId": "2346815014", "name": "Zhong-Xiang Dai"}], "abstract": "Improving every agent in an LLM-based multi-agent system can be prohibitively costly, making it important to identify which agent should be prioritized to achieve the greatest system-level gain with limited resources. Failure attribution can inform this decision, but an agent's responsibility for failures does not directly reveal the system-level gains achievable by improving it. We therefore introduce IF-MAP, an influence-function-inspired method for identifying agents with high potential for system-level improvement. For each agent, IF-MAP measures end-to-end loss changes under controlled semantic prompt perturbations, fits a regularized local response model in a low-dimensional prompt-embedding subspace, and uses the estimated response norm as a priority score. This black-box procedure requires no access to model parameters or gradients and produces agent priorities that can guide subsequent improvement effort. We evaluate IF-MAP across six multi-agent workflows spanning code generation, question answering, and mathematical reasoning, using the same priorities for prompt optimization and model upgrades. Optimizing the selected agents'prompts achieves a macro-average gain of 3.81 percentage points, compared with 1.85 for uniform agent selection. Across five target models, upgrading the selected agents yields macro-average gains of 7.62-16.42 percentage points, compared with 3.81-8.62 for uniform agent selection. Despite its probing overhead, IF-MAP reduces Qwen3-8B and DeepSeek-V3.2 token use by 74.2% and 35.2%, respectively, relative to optimizing every agent, and reduces target-model tokens by 63.6-64.9% relative to upgrading every agent. These results support the use of IF-MAP to identify promising agents and direct improvement effort toward greater end-to-end performance gains."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "6940423e79360933d46ecfb5bd7dd8c2cb57c3b9", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.22791", "CorpusId": 293453387}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6940423e79360933d46ecfb5bd7dd8c2cb57c3b9", "title": "Self-Evolving Coding Agents via Harness-Policy Optimization", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22791, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-16", "authors": [{"authorId": "122835755", "name": "Zhepei Wei"}, {"authorId": "2363427861", "name": "Wen-Lin Yao"}, {"authorId": "2257158371", "name": "Bao-Lin Peng"}, {"authorId": "2254643264", "name": "Dan-Qing Wang"}, {"authorId": "2339987832", "name": "Qian-Hui Wu"}, {"authorId": "2280195943", "name": "Hao Cheng"}, {"authorId": "145391513", "name": "Yu Meng"}, {"authorId": "2256752511", "name": "Jian-Feng Gao"}], "abstract": "Large language model (LLM) agents have shown promising potential in coding tasks, and their performance is jointly determined by the policy model and the harness that mediates their interaction with the environment. Indeed, harness and policy are mutually reinforcing and evolving only one component is suboptimal---a harness tuned for one checkpoint may not target the failure modes of an updated policy, while policy learning remains limited to the trajectories elicited by a fixed harness. In light of this, we propose Self-HPO, a recursive self-improving framework for coding agents that alternates harness and policy optimization, using the evolving policy as its own harness proposer. Specifically, in each round, the current policy first analyzes its own failures and proposes new harness candidates (H-step). The policy is then updated through reinforcement learning (RL) under the selected harness (P-step), and hard tasks identified during training form the next harness search set, keeping harness optimization targeted to the policy's evolving failure modes. Across three coding benchmarks, Self-HPO consistently outperforms harness-only and policy-only baselines, improving Qwen3.5-35B-A3B's average resolve rate from 57.8% to 64.8%, with gains generalizing to out-of-distribution settings. Further analysis shows that both optimized components retain gains even when paired with their base counterparts, while their joint gain exceeds the sum of their individual gains. Self-HPO also improves reliability (Pass@1) and expands the capability frontier (Pass@k) over the base agent. Additionally, we show that with a stronger external model (e.g., Opus 4.8) as the harness proposer, the same framework yields a further performance boost."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "6baa95b99b084f05c311d0285aae5101800c06e6", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.22740", "CorpusId": 293453444}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6baa95b99b084f05c311d0285aae5101800c06e6", "title": "R$^2$Orch: Recursive Self-Improvement for Agent Orchestration via Self-Play within a Skill-Internalization Loop", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22740, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-15", "authors": [{"authorId": "2358581757", "name": "Ming-Da Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2491069700", "name": "Yung-Lin Huang"}, {"authorId": "2499935314", "name": "Qian-Shuo Huang"}, {"authorId": "2505394988", "name": "Yan-Jin Li"}, {"authorId": "47519958", "name": "Rui Qian"}, {"authorId": "2505463762", "name": "Qi-Chao Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2189825927", "name": "Tiesunlong Shen"}, {"authorId": "2514548877", "name": "Na Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2514783533", "name": "Jian Wang"}, {"authorId": "2376127536", "name": "Xiao-Ying Tang"}], "abstract": "In recent years, agentic orchestration has been widely applied to solve complex human tasks. However, existing orchestration learning still faces key challenges, including hand-designed curricula, advantage collapse when a task's rollouts all reach the same outcome, and unreliable evaluation signals that judge each skill by one noisy score. To address these challenges, we propose R$^2$Orch, a new paradigm of Recursive Self-Play Orchestration: an inner loop of self-play that improves the orchestrator on self-chosen tasks, and an outer loop that improves how the inner loop improves. To support this paradigm, we introduce Recursive Learnability, a training objective that rewards a Proposer for choosing tasks whose rollouts keep reaching different outcomes as skills are withdrawn. Built on this objective, we further propose Skill Withdrawal, in which the outer loop withdraws each skill once a controlled evaluation within self-play shows that the orchestrator performs as well without it. Moreover, the two loops form one cycle: failures are distilled into skills, skills into parameters, and withdrawals lead the Proposer to new failures, so the orchestrator moves toward zero-skill deployment. Experimental results on twelve datasets show that R$^2$Orch consistently outperforms baselines across question answering, math, code, and interactive tasks. Our code is available at https://github.com/beita6969/R2Orch."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "eccc0716bef086e99e5430e9b4c72fcd3d817e60", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.22466", "CorpusId": 293453108}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/eccc0716bef086e99e5430e9b4c72fcd3d817e60", "title": "Harness-Delta Attribution: Dissecting Gains From Harness Evolution", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.22466, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-15", "authors": [{"authorId": "2037491", "name": "Wenxuan Ding"}, {"authorId": "2365376107", "name": "Qin Lu"}, {"authorId": "2302894470", "name": "Chang-Long Yu"}, {"authorId": "2405778950", "name": "Sha Li"}, {"authorId": "2244738638", "name": "Shuo-Wei Jin"}, {"authorId": "2362222390", "name": "Xin Liu"}, {"authorId": "2408193613", "name": "Zhen-Yu Shi"}, {"authorId": "2271320260", "name": "Greg Durrett"}], "abstract": "Harness evolution automatically optimizes the prompts, skills, tools, and orchestration code around a fixed-weight model, offering a way to improve agentic systems without updating model weights. The agent harness is iteratively optimized to achieve higher performance on the search set. But how much of this gain reflects benchmark-specific overfitting or additional inference-time compute, and how much may generalize beyond the search set? In this work, we introduce Harness-Delta Attribution (HDA), an evaluation protocol that dissects an evolution gain into artifact-driven overfitting, gains explained by test-time scaling, and the remainder, which gives an upper bound on residual generalizable improvement. We apply HDA to 11 executor models and three harness-evolution frameworks across math, coding, creativity, and agentic tasks. In 21 of these settings that show statistically significant search-set gains, we find that a large share of the improvement is explained by overfitting or test-time scaling. Specifically, among these 21 settings, overfitting and test-time scaling account for 54% and 14% of the gain on average; their combined share exceeds 50% in 14 settings. The residual and held-out transfer vary substantially across tasks, models, and frameworks. Importantly, search-set gains often fail to transfer to held-out tasks, and provide limited evidence that they will persist under practical distribution shifts. Moreover, validation-based signals are insufficient to prevent overfitting. These results motivate attribution-aware evaluation in future harness evolution research."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "d37672893b81fe88028ade1f4166a77a037eeee2", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.19424", "CorpusId": 292902687}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d37672893b81fe88028ade1f4166a77a037eeee2", "title": "VideoHarness-SRSI: Structured Recursive Self-Improvement for Video Understanding Harness", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19424, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-14", "authors": [{"authorId": "2254334449", "name": "Zi-Hao Lin"}, {"authorId": "2309801263", "name": "Hao-Yu Dong"}, {"authorId": "2379955487", "name": "Hao-Bo Xu"}, {"authorId": "2339005997", "name": "Chi-Chih Chang"}, {"authorId": "2266396687", "name": "Xu-Ying Ning"}, {"authorId": "2258547648", "name": "Ying-Long Xia"}, {"authorId": "2504318197", "name": "Shuang Yang"}, {"authorId": "2352021017", "name": "Zhuo-Kai Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2507464273", "name": "Xin-Yi Song"}, {"authorId": "1995922397", "name": "Dongfang Liu"}, {"authorId": "2293354444", "name": "Li-Fu Huang"}, {"authorId": "2393886238", "name": "Yang Song"}, {"authorId": "2266712798", "name": "Qi-Fan Wang"}], "abstract": "LLM system capabilities depend not only on model parameters but also on the executable harnesses that organize information acquisition, tool use, reasoning, and memory. Recursive harness evolution can automate their design, but how it can reliably discover generalizable systems from limited, noisy, and repeatedly reused feedback remains unclear. We study this question in agentic video understanding, where rich multimodal evidence and coupled mechanisms make harness optimization challenging. We first introduce VHarness-BRSI, a recursive self-improvement framework that evolves video harnesses around a frozen vision-language model. Analyzing over 4,000 evolved candidates, we find it competitive with hand-engineered systems but identify three interconnected failure modes: validation overfitting, search-budget dilution, and the early-experience trap. We trace all three to a common root: the same imperfect validation evidence drives both harness revisions and the proposer's search strategy. We therefore propose VHarness-SRSI, an evidence-governed framework in which every search decision rests on evidence that is transferable, sufficient, and gathered before the search commits. It restricts the proposer to content-free feedback, informs exploration with instrumented warm-up experiments, and develops mechanisms in isolated probe-race branches before factorial composition and joint refinement. Across four video understanding benchmarks, VideoHarness-SRSI improves the equally weighted mean held-out score by 6.2% relative to VideoHarness-BRSI and reduces the mean validation-test gap by 37.9%. Reliable harness evolution thus depends on how search is organized and informed, not only on how well it optimizes validation performance."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "842066f2cfe3ade2af69303a0f3a03837035b44a", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.19951", "CorpusId": 292903495}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/842066f2cfe3ade2af69303a0f3a03837035b44a", "title": "AtomSearch: Evidence-Grounded Tree Search for Agent Skill Optimization", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19951, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-14", "authors": [{"authorId": "2497901572", "name": "Jiang-Shuai-Chen-Rui-,-Zhang-Rong-,-Wang-Gui-Fang- Tong"}, {"authorId": "2430689890", "name": "Sen Deng"}, {"authorId": "2469306138", "name": "Ming-Pei Peng"}, {"authorId": "2311866886", "name": "Jian-Feng Pan"}], "abstract": "Agent skills adapt frozen models through editable external instructions. Revising several rules at once makes changes difficult to inspect, while judging each local edit only by its immediate score can discard useful intermediate states. We introduce AtomSearch, which keeps revision actions local while using subsequent skill states to guide search. Contrastive atomic editing links each primitive rule edit to failed and successful executions, a behavioral hypothesis, and an intended scope. Skill-State Tree Search (STS) evaluates short edit compositions and selectively retains them as executable states that can be revisited and extended. Outcome memory supplies later proposals with observed repair and regression statistics. We evaluate AtomSearch across four tasks and five target models. With deepseek-v4-flash, it exceeds SkillOpt on three of four tasks, including a 9.68-percentage-point gain on LiveMathematicianBench, and SkillOpt's final skills contain 2.3-19.7 times as many characters. In 67% of completed searches, the best validation state passes through an ancestor that was not highest-scoring at its depth. These results show how local rule edits can support the exploration of useful revision paths and yield compact skills, with additional offline evaluations used to explore alternative states."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "5f96f13818e02002e3bd673e414b9ea9ea77ad73", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.19799", "CorpusId": 292902670}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/5f96f13818e02002e3bd673e414b9ea9ea77ad73", "title": "Evolve Together, Fall into Place: How Social Scaffolding Shapes Self-Evolving Multi-Agent Systems", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.19799, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-14", "authors": [{"authorId": "2306192851", "name": "Seojin Hwang"}, {"authorId": "2506643974", "name": "Y. Choi"}, {"authorId": "2311744808", "name": "Hwanhee Lee"}], "abstract": "Recent work lets LLM agents evolve their own harness, up to the team itself. Yet in human groups, the same abilities achieve different outcomes under different social conditions. Borrowing the notion of scaffolding from developmental psychology, we investigate whether the social scaffolding of a self-evolving multi-agent system (MAS), namely its team composition and structure of interdependence (e.g., collaboration, conformity, or competition), shapes how the team evolves. We present AGORA (Agents Growing through Organization and Relational Arrangements), a framework that varies the two axes independently across three evolution mechanisms. Across web search, codebase understanding, and document QA, we find that (1) evolution alone rarely helps, while the right scaffolding surpasses a strong agent evolved alone by up to 25 points; (2) it does so by moving authority to the most capable member while the others step back; and (3) this member, having grown within the team, outperforms the same model alone. Designing a self-evolving MAS thus requires designing its social scaffolding, which shapes not only how its agents learn, but who gets to lead."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "d0da293bdf2aebff1ff751baabf4d94685436872", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.17613", "CorpusId": 292896378}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/d0da293bdf2aebff1ff751baabf4d94685436872", "title": "MAS-Gate: Unlocking Multi-Agent System Lifecycle via Node-Centric Quantification", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.17613, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-13", "authors": [{"authorId": "2260832033", "name": "He-Hai Lin"}, {"authorId": "2342357003", "name": "Zi-Xuan Wang"}, {"authorId": "2510560117", "name": "Ming Yan"}, {"authorId": "2282963978", "name": "Pei-Dong Wang"}, {"authorId": "2306768858", "name": "Shi-Lei Cao"}, {"authorId": "2261446627", "name": "Min-Zhi Li"}, {"authorId": "2064493724", "name": "Bosheng Ding"}, {"authorId": "50844234", "name": "Jue-Peng Zheng"}, {"authorId": "2383968687", "name": "Cheng-Wei Qin"}], "abstract": "Large language model (LLM)-based multi-agent systems (MAS) can solve complex problems beyond the reach of a single agent by decomposing tasks and coordinating complementary capabilities. Despite extensive research on MAS, existing work largely focuses on specific aspects or individual phases of their development and operation, resulting in fragmented perspectives with objectives and signals that are difficult to reconcile across these different settings. To bridge these disconnected perspectives, we conceptualize MAS development and operation as a three-phase lifecycle spanning design-time orchestration before execution, runtime intervention during execution, and post-hoc attribution after execution, and introduce MAS-Gate, a unified node-centric quantification applicable across these phases. Modeling each execution as a directed acyclic graph, MAS-Gate combines within-node utility, which estimates a node's contribution through changes in reference-answer likelihood, with inter-node strength, which approximates semantic uptake and potential downstream reach. We adapt this shared quantification to three objectives: providing a structure-aware dense reward for Orchestrator training, training an Editor to act on execution prefixes using paired rewards from completed edited and unedited trajectories, and supplying risk-ranked nodes as auxiliary evidence for LLM-based post-hoc attribution. Experiments spanning interaction environments, knowledge-intensive multi-hop question answering, general reasoning, and post-hoc attribution demonstrate that MAS-Gate improves workflow construction, targeted runtime editing, and LLM-assisted error localization over corresponding phase-specific baselines. Together, these results establish MAS-Gate as a unified structural interface for improving MAS generation, dynamic editing, and error attribution throughout the lifecycle."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "dcc2fbfbc8fdb2f8c8b0b29d6bb08bbc4e3933c7", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.18813", "CorpusId": 292894673}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/dcc2fbfbc8fdb2f8c8b0b29d6bb08bbc4e3933c7", "title": "Self-Evolving Agent Harness", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.18813, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-13", "authors": [{"authorId": "2174671074", "name": "Chu-Yang Wei"}, {"authorId": "2333357871", "name": "Hao-Xiang Guan"}, {"authorId": "2203918573", "name": "Ming-Hang Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2434954462", "name": "Ken Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2367749242", "name": "Ji-Yan He"}, {"authorId": "46223995", "name": "Xi-Ren Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2468000469", "name": "Huan-Huan Chen"}, {"authorId": "2461908277", "name": "Yi-Fei Shen"}], "abstract": "Harness self-evolution revises an agent's software scaffolding, including prompts, memory, workflows, and executable components, while keeping model weights fixed. We formalize a feedback-driven process in which accepted revisions persist across runs. The framework separates update targets from proposal and admission mechanisms. It distinguishes changes to interpreted state from revisions to tools and code, repository-level control logic, and plugin composition. We characterize search through evolutionary optimization and examine its reliability through continual PAC-learning. Under explicit assumptions, the analysis yields an expected generalization bound, a retention bound conditional on historical regression budgets, and a high-probability admission rule using fresh, independent validation data. We examine how persistent harness revisions affect knowledge sharing and organizational workflows, with implications for scientific reliability, physical safety, and institutional accountability. Cases from prior work illustrate model--harness co-design, distributed execution, and plugin reuse. Reliable deployment requires continual validation as task distributions and evaluators change, with independent safeguards for learning, runtime privileges, and consequential actions. We provide a comprehensive 66-page discussion of related work, theory, algorithms, evaluation, applications, and the broader impact of Harness RSI, together with continuously updated code and supplementary materials, at https://github.com/zgcagi/Harness-RSI."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "9cf4f144bb261f0e2914f14a85fd3aa63288f2f2", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.18069", "CorpusId": 292897457}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/9cf4f144bb261f0e2914f14a85fd3aa63288f2f2", "title": "From Agents to Edges: Paired Optimization for Multi-Agent Collaboration", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.18069, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-13", "authors": [{"authorId": "2280850617", "name": "Feng Lin"}, {"authorId": "2281003213", "name": "Xiao-Tian Yu"}, {"authorId": "2280332748", "name": "Yi-Fan Yang"}, {"authorId": "2509633962", "name": "A. Li"}], "abstract": "Strong individual agents do not guarantee effective collaboration. Communication must align what one agent conveys with how another interprets and uses it. This dependency makes the communication edge a natural unit of optimization. We introduce Paired Automated Communication Optimization (PACO), which jointly optimizes the instructions at both ends of each edge and their shared communication convention in systems built on black-box language models. PACO keeps model weights, agent roles, and workflow topology fixed. A state-aware discounted bandit selects edges for refinement, while execution feedback guides paired revisions evaluated through staged end-to-end execution. The optimized instructions integrate into existing agent prompts without additional model calls at deployment. On a four-agent workflow evaluated across diverse multi-agent benchmarks, PACO consistently outperforms leading prompt-optimization and communication baselines. Configurations learned on HotpotQA retain their advantage in cross-dataset transfer without target-specific optimization. These results demonstrate that optimizing how agents communicate can substantially improve collaboration within an otherwise fixed system."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "7baa4fa876ba953f9f1f9468105602cea42c48af", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.17370", "CorpusId": 292896173}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/7baa4fa876ba953f9f1f9468105602cea42c48af", "title": "HarnessTTT: Learn to Optimize Harness at Test-Time for Scientific Discovery", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.17370, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-12", "authors": [{"authorId": "2269426296", "name": "Ying-Zi Ma"}, {"authorId": "2146176464", "name": "Yu-Long Cao"}, {"authorId": "49088449", "name": "Nanxi Li"}, {"authorId": "2359179354", "name": "Fang Wu"}, {"authorId": "48378494", "name": "Yi-Zhe Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2347006409", "name": "James Y. Zou"}, {"authorId": "2382829310", "name": "Ye-Jin Choi"}, {"authorId": "2273519476", "name": "Chao-Wei Xiao"}], "abstract": "Scientific discovery with language models requires finding the best valid solution within a finite computational budget. We study how feedback from this search can improve the procedure used to conduct it. We introduce HarnessTTT, a test-time learning framework that trains a harness proposer while keeping the executor frozen. The proposer generates executable harnesses comprising system prompts, reusable skills, tools, middleware, and execution controls. Each harness guides a budgeted discovery rollout, whose outcome provides feedback for updating the proposer. A discovery-oriented policy-gradient objective combines gap-normalized rewards, leave-one-out advantages (RLOO), and max-sharpening to favor high-performing proposals. Generated components are validated before execution, and the search retains the best valid solution and selected harnesses across rounds. Across fourteen discovery tasks in mathematics, systems, GPU kernels, heuristic programming, astrodynamics, and biology, HarnessTTT with a frozen 9B executor improves on OpenEvolve with the same executor on all eleven benchmark tasks (e.g., from 1.17 to 2.64 on circle packing), improves on the discovery-fine-tuned Finch-9B by up to 92%, holds the best open-weight sub-10B result on nine, and reaches 0.380919 on Erd\\H{o}s minimum overlap, better than the best human reference of 0.380927. Scaling the proposer and frozen executor to 27B improves on eleven of fourteen tasks, and transferring the learned harnesses to GPT-5.5 matches or exceeds the strongest reported system of any backbone on nine of fourteen tasks. Together, these results show that harness search can itself be learned at test time: a trained proposer lets a small frozen executor search effectively, and the harnesses it finds carry over to stronger executors."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "13f98adf0521aa1c56096c60b1aa67af59345671", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.16491", "CorpusId": 292896228}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/13f98adf0521aa1c56096c60b1aa67af59345671", "title": "Drafting Agents, Not Just Tokens: Speculative Recursive Self-Improvement for Agentic Systems", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.16491, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-12", "authors": [{"authorId": "2392974873", "name": "Xin Xu"}, {"authorId": "2411060706", "name": "Ke Cheng"}, {"authorId": "2144221741", "name": "Hao Wang"}, {"authorId": "2410885767", "name": "Yang-Ru Huang"}, {"authorId": "2116643796", "name": "Yang-Hao Xiao"}, {"authorId": "2162399554", "name": "Zhi-Chao Chen"}, {"authorId": "2294147935", "name": "Zhi-Sheng Zhong"}, {"authorId": "2330434826", "name": "Xun Liu"}, {"authorId": "2510733535", "name": "Yi Li"}, {"authorId": "2276745414", "name": "Qian Li"}, {"authorId": "38929912", "name": "Hai-Bin Lv"}, {"authorId": "2394312357", "name": "Jun Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2317576627", "name": "Jie Jiang"}, {"authorId": "2346896471", "name": "Zhou-Chen Lin"}], "abstract": "Improving an agent through successive revisions requires balancing exploration and evaluation under a limited budget. A narrow search can miss useful sequences of edits, while fully evaluating every intermediate version often exhausts the budget before those sequences yield a verified improvement. Inspired by the draft--verify principle of speculative decoding, we propose Speculative Recursive Self-Improvement (SpecRSI), a two-level framework coupling inner-loop agent search with outer-loop improver learning. SpecRSI drafts successors before their predecessors are fully evaluated, while adopting only fully evaluated, admissible versions. In the inner loop, Verification-Aware Tree Search (VATS) allocates the budget between further edits and outstanding checks using the predicted verified return of a continuation. In the outer loop, Group Verification Optimization (GVO) updates the improver from completed searches while keeping the real evaluator fixed. We evaluate SpecRSI on scientific optimization (including active queue management (AQM) for network infrastructure), coding, and general agent-system benchmarks. Under matched evaluation-call budgets, SpecRSI improves scientific terminal gain over Janus by 60.0%--96.3% and area under the budget curve by 32.1%--91.6%, while achieving higher means across all 50 scientific backbone--task pairs and outperforming MetaRSI-v1 by 1.30--2.70 percentage points (pp) in system score across twenty backbones. On instance-level comparisons, it further improves PyBaMM terminal gain by 142.6% and SWE-Pro success by 6.0 pp at 7.0% and 9.2% lower token cost, respectively. These results demonstrate the efficacy of the proposed approach."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "c19d06c396ecb9a53a01bc8a83feace3a4148921", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.15915", "CorpusId": 292894504}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/c19d06c396ecb9a53a01bc8a83feace3a4148921", "title": "VIEWCRAFT: Evolving What Multimodal Agents See and How They Read It", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15915, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-11", "authors": [{"authorId": "2375625516", "name": "Bin-Xiao Xu"}, {"authorId": "2407465447", "name": "Rui-Chuan An"}, {"authorId": "2269737481", "name": "Bocheng Zou"}, {"authorId": "2214830561", "name": "Weihua Du"}, {"authorId": "2276843415", "name": "Xin-Yu Wei"}, {"authorId": "2405115995", "name": "Hang-Yu Guo"}, {"authorId": "2510650275", "name": "Yi-Fan Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2147420753", "name": "Ren-Ye Yan"}, {"authorId": "2326302007", "name": "Chengqi Duan"}, {"authorId": "2331326371", "name": "Hang Hua"}], "abstract": "Reusable skills improve multimodal agents without updating model weights, but their effectiveness depends on both the evidence presented and the procedure used to read it. We introduce ViewCraft, which jointly evolves an executable evidence interface and its reading procedure. The interface selects, transforms, and organizes observations; the reader interprets the resulting views. Failure traces guide revisions, replay and paired evaluation retain useful changes, and interface projection recovers visual edits from eligible rejected joint proposals. Across eight benchmarks, ViewCraft improves over direct execution by an average of $6.1$ points in task score, with gains on every benchmark. The advantage persists at a matched input-token budget. On all five multi-source tasks, the learned interface uses 8%-15% fewer input tokens than Initial skill. The learned skills also transfer across executors and outperform direct execution in every evaluated transfer, supporting evidence construction and reading as a coupled, reusable skill."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "5067c9be45c28716c1b254b4906c85aaf1f964f6", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.15788", "CorpusId": 292895518}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/5067c9be45c28716c1b254b4906c85aaf1f964f6", "title": "Agent Sociodrama: Social-Dynamics-Guided Persona Recasting for LLM Agent Teams", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15788, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-11", "authors": [{"authorId": "2376151962", "name": "Yi-Rui Qi"}, {"authorId": "2290856471", "name": "Xiao-Ming Zhang"}, {"authorId": "13563486", "name": "Jaehong Yoon"}], "abstract": "Large language model (LLM) agents are increasingly deployed as teams, yet they fail in characteristically social ways: information holders stay silent, dominant speakers anchor groups on wrong answers, and norm violations go unsanctioned. We present Agent Sociodrama, a framework for modeling and validating evolving social dynamics in LLM agent teams. Under fixed personas, agents'emotional states and directed, asymmetric relationships are updated every round by a unified external LLM judge and shape their subsequent interactions. Across six LLM backbones and five social scenarios, we assess social fidelity through classical social-theory patterns and agreement with human judgments. The resulting trajectories exhibit structured social patterns and model-specific differences. We then introduce Trajectory-Guided Persona Recasting (TPR), which diagnoses group-dynamic failures from interaction transcripts and social trajectories and recasts agent personas before rerunning the task. Across four collaborative benchmarks, TPR improves performance from both heterogeneous and randomly initialized agent persona compositions, yielding up to 83.3%p of accuracy improvement over the initial casting. On representative distributed-information cooperation and mixed-motive bargaining tasks, we outperform SOTOPIA and AgentVerse in three of four comparisons, by up to 37.5%p in Pareto rate. These results show that validated social trajectories provide actionable signals for repairing LLM agent teams."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "1fbacea7d3c9402ba784b7cee343b76b18bbeffe", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.14481", "CorpusId": 292859217}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/1fbacea7d3c9402ba784b7cee343b76b18bbeffe", "title": "Optimize Once, Execute Fast: Latency-Aware Multi-Agent Workflow Learning for Recurrent Queries", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14481, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-10", "authors": [{"authorId": "2363502897", "name": "En-Pei Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2395673580", "name": "Fei-Yu Qu"}, {"authorId": "2348185780", "name": "Zheng Huang"}, {"authorId": "2356800731", "name": "Da-Wei Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2363405945", "name": "Elynn Chen"}, {"authorId": "2407905820", "name": "Yu-Jun Yan"}], "abstract": "Large Language Model-based Multi-Agent Systems (LLM-based MAS) have shown remarkable success in solving complex tasks by coordinating specialized agents through multi-step workflows. To overcome the high cost and specificity of manually designed workflows, recent research has shifted toward learning them automatically. However, a key limitation remains: while agent calls introduce significant latency due to model inference time and service congestion, most automated methods optimize solely for downstream performance, ignoring execution time (makespan). This issue is amplified when workflows are reused across recurring queries, making latency accumulate over time. This limits deployment in recurrent applications such as AI customer support and AI cloud troubleshooting, where users expect fast responses. To bridge this gap, we introduce a new problem, termed latency-aware workflow generation, where the objective is to minimize workflow makespan while aiming to retain downstream performance. We propose LAWA (Latency-Aware Workflow optimizAtion), a novel framework that reduces makespan via theoretically grounded graph edits targeting critical-path bottlenecks. By unifying a graph generator with this graph-editing procedure, LAWA supports both de novo workflow generation and refinement of existing ones. Empirical results across seven benchmarks demonstrate that LAWA significantly reduces makespan while achieving competitive task performance, supporting efficient reuse of multi-agent workflows for recurrent queries."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "dce4deeae7b56fe7dca0d3fa4ba76b890a6329f8", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.14977", "CorpusId": 292895073}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/dce4deeae7b56fe7dca0d3fa4ba76b890a6329f8", "title": "EnactOpt: Diagnosis-Guided Harness Evolution for Embodied Agents", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14977, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-10", "authors": [{"authorId": "2406050612", "name": "Zhi-Yuan Feng"}, {"authorId": "2352886718", "name": "Zhuo-Shi Pan"}, {"authorId": "2392723980", "name": "Zhao-Lu Kang"}, {"authorId": "2510533300", "name": "Zhong-Xia Chen"}, {"authorId": "2402763425", "name": "Shuo Yang"}, {"authorId": "2510556629", "name": "S. Ye"}, {"authorId": "2510662756", "name": "Heng Wang"}, {"authorId": "2506266474", "name": "Han-Yu Li"}], "abstract": "Embodied agents built on foundation models rely on an execution harness to organize observations, guide actions, and incorporate execution feedback into subsequent decisions. Failures can arise not only from limited model capabilities, but also from how the harness presents evidence and guides its use. These model-external mechanisms provide an adaptation target without updating model parameters, but task-specific interaction experience can be costly to collect. Task-level outcomes alone do not reveal whether relevant evidence was missing from the model input or was available but inadequately used. Moreover, these two aspects are coupled: effective action guidance may require evidence that the current context omits, while exposing that evidence alone does not ensure an appropriate response. We introduce EnactOpt, which uses limited execution feedback to jointly evolve reusable textual skills and context-construction code while keeping the underlying models and runtime fixed. It diagnoses adjacent decisions by relating action feedback to the actual model input and subsequent action. Structured episode summaries guide joint revisions through prompt-level edit operators, and candidate harnesses undergo compatibility checks and validation-based retention. Across VLABench, ALFWorld, and Embodied Agent Interface (EAI), EnactOpt improves task success by approximately 10-27 percentage points over the initial harnesses and outperforms the evaluated GEPA, TextGrad, and Trace2Skill baselines. These gains extend to task conditions not used for harness evolution or candidate selection. The results support a complementary route to embodied adaptation: turning limited execution experience into reusable behavioral improvements by jointly refining how evidence is presented and acted upon."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "7764f11267ea93764232cece7a844059352b304a", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.15150", "CorpusId": 292895781}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/7764f11267ea93764232cece7a844059352b304a", "title": "Harmony Harness: Multi-Objective Self-Evolution of Agent Harnesses", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.15150, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-10", "authors": [{"authorId": "2258712398", "name": "Wei-Cheng Yu"}, {"authorId": "71436125", "name": "Lesly Miculicich"}, {"authorId": "40135250", "name": "Bhavana Dalvi"}, {"authorId": "2155099104", "name": "Jie-Feng Chen"}, {"authorId": "2501916210", "name": "Shuo Li"}, {"authorId": "2337690647", "name": "Matt Fredrikson"}, {"authorId": "2305619900", "name": "Tomas Pfister"}, {"authorId": "2253355959", "name": "Long T. Le"}], "abstract": "Recent work on self-evolving LLM agents treats the agent harness as an optimization target that can be iteratively improved through execution feedback. Existing approaches, however, largely optimize a single objective and evolve the harness as a monolithic artifact, limiting their ability to handle competing goals and to separate transferable design from environment-specific adaptation. We introduce Harmony Harness, a framework for multi-objective, compositional harness evolution. Harmony Harness formulates harness evolution as a multi-objective optimization problem and uses decomposition-based evolutionary search to maintain solutions across different regions of the Pareto frontier, avoiding reliance on a hand-designed structural diversity metric for repository-scale harnesses. In parallel, we introduce factorized evolution operators that separately refine general-purpose harness mechanisms and benchmark-specific adaptations, together with a dynamic composition workflow that integrates them at inference time. Across SecRepoBench and BaxBench, Harmony Harness consistently outperforms strong harness-evolution baselines, with gains exceeding 10% on both benchmarks. Our results show that explicitly modeling both objective trade-offs and harness structure yields substantially stronger and more transferable agent evolution than existing single-objective, monolithic approaches."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "6b277d2fd5d47e365dd1ffcf7c673caf915b253a", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.13064", "CorpusId": 292829136}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6b277d2fd5d47e365dd1ffcf7c673caf915b253a", "title": "CB-Orchestrator: Adaptive Workflow Optimization for LLM Agents via Contextual Bandits", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.13064, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-09", "authors": [{"authorId": "2343681730", "name": "Jia-Hang Sun"}, {"authorId": "2382918145", "name": "Zhi-Wei Shang"}, {"authorId": "2507857055", "name": "Zhi-Piao Liu"}, {"authorId": "2507863263", "name": "Hong-Wei Yang"}, {"authorId": "2507864943", "name": "Guo-Qing Xie"}, {"authorId": "2383826566", "name": "Shuang Qiu"}, {"authorId": "2346815014", "name": "Zhong-Xiang Dai"}], "abstract": "Large Language Model (LLM) agents have demonstrated remarkable capabilities in tackling complex tasks through agentic workflows. However, manually designing these workflows is labor-intensive and lacks flexibility to adapt to diverse queries. While automated workflow optimization is a promising direction, existing methods often incur expensive API costs during training-phase feedback collection. Furthermore, they fail to adaptively reuse high-quality workflows, which creates a bottleneck for both performance and cost-effectiveness. To address these limitations, we propose CB-Orchestrator, an adaptive framework that decouples workflow generation from selection. In the first stage, we construct a diverse pool of candidate workflows via evolutionary search. In the second stage, we formulate workflow selection as a Contextual Bandit problem, which enables sample-efficient learning by balancing exploration and exploitation, thereby significantly reducing the feedback required for training. During inference, the model adaptively selects the optimal workflow tailored to each query. Evaluations across five diverse benchmarks demonstrate that CB-Orchestrator consistently outperforms all baselines. Notably, on the MATH dataset, CB-Orchestrator achieves better performance than strong recent baselines while reducing training-phase API token overhead by no less than 40.55% and total end-to-end training (including workflow pool generation) token overhead by no less than 29.73%."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "8575a1cec19b396978efa53eed7bff1822032158", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.12927", "CorpusId": 292828592}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/8575a1cec19b396978efa53eed7bff1822032158", "title": "Collaborative Learning and Reasoning over Model Slices", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.12927, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-09", "authors": [{"authorId": "2379583715", "name": "Yu-Heng Wang"}, {"authorId": "2379057166", "name": "Run-De Yang"}, {"authorId": "2445701562", "name": "Zhu-Han Bao"}, {"authorId": "77005311", "name": "Qi-Ran Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2378579318", "name": "Hua-Tao Li"}, {"authorId": "2509371498", "name": "Ying Zheng"}, {"authorId": "2265075699", "name": "Lin-Na Wu"}, {"authorId": "2347555604", "name": "Jing-Ru Fan"}, {"authorId": "2283880758", "name": "Shun-Yu Yao"}, {"authorId": "2388116018", "name": "Yi-Han Li"}, {"authorId": "2377174814", "name": "Zhi Li"}, {"authorId": "2404422283", "name": "Chen Qian"}], "abstract": "Large language models (LLMs) excel across diverse tasks, benefiting from knowledge priors, in-context reasoning, and test-time computation. Despite these strengths, single-model reasoning remains vulnerable to error propagation, as later steps depend on the model's earlier outputs, leaving reasoning blind spots difficult to independently reassess. To address this limitation, we develop \\textit{collaborative learning}, encompassing the construction of heterogeneous collaborators and the learning of effective cross-model collaboration. Specifically, \\textit{model slicing} replaces a large model with smaller, heterogeneous models under a comparable parameter budget, enabling prior reasoning to be reassessed across distinct model parameterizations. \\textit{Pyramid supervision} combines protocol distillation with step-level supervision to refine reasoning and interaction decisions throughout collaboration. We evaluate our approach across four task categories: multi-hop question answering, mathematical reasoning, code generation, and open-ended constrained generation. The trained heterogeneous models achieve comparable or better task performance than the original large model and established multi-agent baselines, including reinforcement-learning-based methods, while also achieving higher inference efficiency than the multi-agent baselines and exhibiting differentiated collaboration patterns across tasks. These findings show that learned collaboration turns model heterogeneity into complementary reasoning capabilities."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "6db864a0e63960d4ddc8a5f234400cbc2fd9f71f", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.13144", "CorpusId": 292828764}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6db864a0e63960d4ddc8a5f234400cbc2fd9f71f", "title": "Competence-Aware Orchestration for LLM-Based Multi-Agent Systems", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.13144, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-09", "authors": [{"authorId": "2362382656", "name": "Song-Miao Wang"}, {"authorId": "2382826931", "name": "Yu-Cheng Wang"}, {"authorId": "2266813865", "name": "Cheng-Lin Yu"}, {"authorId": "2383948719", "name": "Hong-Xia Yang"}, {"authorId": "2362514471", "name": "Ming Li"}], "abstract": "LLM-based multi-agent systems (MAS) have emerged as a promising paradigm for enhancing complex reasoning through role specialization and collaboration, yet a key challenge remains: how can collaboration be adapted to a backbone's competence on the current query? This problem matters because more collaboration is not always better--richer coordination may help on difficult cases, but it also incurs additional token cost and redundant interactions. We propose a two-stage competence-aware framework for automatic MAS orchestration. It first models the LLM backbone's task-conditioned competence for the current query, and then uses this signal to adapt role activation and the communication graph. Across six benchmarks, our method achieves an average score of 93.81%. Experiments with six LLM backbones of varying competence levels further show that our method adjusts collaboration according to the inferred competence: for higher-competence backbones, it improves average accuracy from 87.22 to 89.97 while reducing token cost by 36.26%; for lower-competence backbones, it yields a further average gain of 4.58 points over existing methods. The same trend also holds on unseen backbones."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "e66668ac21cc88ae8156b5f8df2d028787f7b92a", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.13494", "CorpusId": 292830900}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/e66668ac21cc88ae8156b5f8df2d028787f7b92a", "title": "Humanize MLE Agents: Multi-Agent Session Alternation Beyond Single-Agent Limits", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.13494, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-09", "authors": [{"authorId": "2305685401", "name": "Chang-Ye Li"}, {"authorId": "2454970662", "name": "Zi-Jian Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2155379137", "name": "Mu-Heng Li"}, {"authorId": "2509965550", "name": "Yi-Duo Pang"}, {"authorId": "2297267780", "name": "Ting-Jun Wu"}, {"authorId": "2449361027", "name": "Meng-Han Li"}, {"authorId": "2506273967", "name": "Yi-Tong Liu"}, {"authorId": "2510022949", "name": "Hong-Kai Guo"}, {"authorId": "2505034681", "name": "Meng Chen"}, {"authorId": "2508870886", "name": "Chen-Yu He"}, {"authorId": "2294380229", "name": "Kai-Sheng Ma"}, {"authorId": "2261365782", "name": "Wei Xu"}, {"authorId": "2283171540", "name": "Song Han"}, {"authorId": "50152441", "name": "Si-Hao Liu"}, {"authorId": "20515689", "name": "Li-Geng Zhu"}], "abstract": "Existing LLM-based agent approaches for long-horizon machine learning engineering (MLE) often use context engineering, implemented through custom harnesses that manage tools, skills, and accumulated experience. However, we find that as single-session execution lengthens, the agent's reasoning intensity declines alongside slowing progress, summarized as long-horizon deceleration, and the benefits of these custom harnesses vanish as the underlying model improves, summarized as harness staleness. These findings suggest that their benefits may not persist across versions, which motivates a workflow-level approach in which agents build on one another's work across sessions. We model these alternating agent sessions as a context-reset semi-Markov decision process (CR-SMDP) and establish sufficient conditions for alternation to exceed single-agent reference limits, derive an explicit experiment-budget bound, and characterize when session caps preserve this advantage. Guided by this analysis, we introduce Humanize MLE Agents (HMA), which alternates two agents on a shared workspace, limits each session to at most k experiments and resets the context between sessions, without additional task-specific harness or prior knowledge supplements. Across 75 Kaggle tasks in MLE-bench, HMA reaches a state-of-the-art any-medal rate of 78.2%, exceeding the strongest open-source baseline by 8.0 points with a quarter of its time budget. Moreover, among six configurations spanning five heterogeneous agent pairs, HMA improves any-medal rate over the constituent baselines by 6.4 points on average, demonstrating cross-pair generality. A supplementary case on kernel optimization reaches 878 cycles, within 1.6% of the community leaderboard leader, demonstrating applicability beyond MLE."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "3cd355c9600b0232a89e6d0f5257c40db74f9299", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.13361", "CorpusId": 292830518}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/3cd355c9600b0232a89e6d0f5257c40db74f9299", "title": "AgentSWE: Can Coding Agents Build the Agent You Actually Want?", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.13361, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-09", "authors": [{"authorId": "2445720410", "name": "Jia-Hao Wang"}, {"authorId": "2374412422", "name": "Hong-Jin Qian"}, {"authorId": "2398804888", "name": "Yuyang Hu"}, {"authorId": "2321653170", "name": "Jia-Jun Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2362012611", "name": "Zheng Liu"}], "abstract": "As the production of software passes into the hands of software itself, coding agents are increasingly commissioned to build agents fitted to their users'own workflows. This delegation carries a quiet assumption, that the delivered agent will faithfully serve the requirement it was built for, rather than merely appear to. No existing benchmark tests that assumption. We introduce AgentSWE, a benchmark for agent software engineering spanning three stages of the agent development lifecycle: Creation of a complete agent from a natural-language requirement, Editing of production agent codebases, and Optimization toward a measurable target. Each of its 25 tasks is framed as a commission rather than an exam: the builder iterates against a handful of illustrative cases, while acceptance exercises the frozen submission on held-out cases designed to span the full requirement, grading behavior against requirement-derived rubrics under hard pass/fail gates or, for Optimization, against the source benchmark's own objective. Evaluating a range of coding agents across models and harnesses, we find that none comes close to delivering what a requirement asks for: the gap between what a builder demonstrates and what it delivers is the norm, not the corner case. An accompanying empirical analysis of the failure trajectories identifies recurring failure modes, among them overfitting to the visible cases, hollow runtimes behind code that looks complete, and unsupported claims of completion, each a signal by which coding agents can learn to build the agents they are asked for."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "cbf4bfb4cf5900d4efa54d7d77ab2619fcde149c", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.13870", "CorpusId": 292830834}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/cbf4bfb4cf5900d4efa54d7d77ab2619fcde149c", "title": "Ask Your Advisor: Learning When to Consult Stronger Models in Agentic Systems", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.13870, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-09", "authors": [{"authorId": "2509953036", "name": "Pascal Sowietzki"}, {"authorId": "2297189635", "name": "Steffen Holter"}, {"authorId": "1414641800", "name": "Yannick Metz"}, {"authorId": "1518623384", "name": "E. Kasper"}, {"authorId": "2228208206", "name": "Raphael Baur"}, {"authorId": "2258965125", "name": "Mennatallah El-Assady"}], "abstract": "To balance task performance and computational cost, agentic workflows can use a lower-cost model for execution and selectively consult a stronger model. In such executor-advisor systems, the executor decides when to seek advice, making escalation depend on self-assessment, although current language models struggle to detect where their own reasoning fails. The need for stronger reasoning emerges during execution and varies across workflow phases. We introduce ARIA, a meta-controller that learns when to consult by balancing task success against consultation cost. Our approach formulates consultation as a sequential resource-allocation problem. At workflow contact points, ARIA maps execution evidence to a compact state abstraction and selects SILENT or CONSULT, with action values learned by Monte Carlo control from cost-penalized outcomes. Across evaluated tasks of SWE-bench Verified, Terminal-Bench 2.1, and $\\tau^2$-bench, ARIA recovers 44-81% of the executor-advisor success gap at less than half the advisor model's cost. ARIA achieves the best observed success-cost trade-off among ten other evaluated conditions, including routing and cascading methods, fixed and prompted consultation, and random placement at matched advice volume. The policies are compact and inspectable, with a single consultation-price parameter governing the success-cost trade-off without retraining either underlying model."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "6fac819b95eb96e9bf74c44e8fe102e48b084d73", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.14224", "CorpusId": 292859473}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6fac819b95eb96e9bf74c44e8fe102e48b084d73", "title": "Harness^2: Recursive Agent Harnessing for an Open World", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.14224, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-09", "authors": [{"authorId": "2319451284", "name": "Rui-Yao Xu"}, {"authorId": "2258603407", "name": "Yan-Fei Chen"}, {"authorId": "2220095956", "name": "Zhongying CuiZhu"}, {"authorId": "40135250", "name": "Bhavana Dalvi"}, {"authorId": "2321601631", "name": "Yifei Ming"}, {"authorId": "2453417135", "name": "Han Yu"}, {"authorId": "2325953181", "name": "Ru-Jun Han"}, {"authorId": "2278969944", "name": "Chen-Yu Lee"}, {"authorId": "2305619900", "name": "Tomas Pfister"}], "abstract": "LLM agents'capabilities depend not only on their underlying models but also on their execution harnesses. Recursive self-improvement (RSI) offers a promising approach to automatically evolve these harnesses. However, deploying RSI in open-world settings still faces two critical challenges. First, open-world tasks are typically unseen during the harness design phase and novel at test time, leaving no ground-truth verifiers to reliably guide online harness edits. Second, the RSI process remains highly task-specific and fails to generalize to novel scenarios. We introduce Harness^2, a plug-and-play, recursive agent harnessing framework that addresses these challenges through two connected levels of recursion. Task-level recursion refines the current task's harness using a contrastive Propose-Probe-Compose step that reliably guides edits without ground-truth verifiers, and can be scaled further via parallel or sequential recursion. Harnessing-level recursion then extracts experience from these edits to update a continual improvement harness, enabling the agent to better adapt to future tasks. Across three professional benchmarks and four model-harness configurations, a single step consistently improves GLM-5 and Gemini 3.5 Flash over their base harnesses, yielding gains of up to 11.5 points on WorkBuddy-Bench, 4.5 points on JobBench, and 11.8 percentage points on LAB, with further gains as the task-level recursion budget increases."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "95625ce4dbfb9d50f43d1fbeee5568962e323ef7", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.11966", "CorpusId": 292737964}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/95625ce4dbfb9d50f43d1fbeee5568962e323ef7", "title": "MindFlow: Mind Supernet Powered Thinking Flows for Research Idea Innovation", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.11966, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-08", "authors": [{"authorId": "2345180782", "name": "Meng-Di Liu"}, {"authorId": "2395563571", "name": "Wen-Jue Chen"}, {"authorId": "2509333041", "name": "Wen-Yue Chen"}, {"authorId": "2383101868", "name": "Cheng Yang"}, {"authorId": "2305774655", "name": "Fan-Qi Kong"}, {"authorId": "1962824654", "name": "Zhang-Yang Gao"}, {"authorId": "2149479237", "name": "Xiao-Xue Cheng"}, {"authorId": "2509367464", "name": "Yi-Heng Li"}, {"authorId": "2505375456", "name": "Yu-Jian Yuan"}, {"authorId": "2288319089", "name": "Ke-Liang Li"}, {"authorId": "2341306489", "name": "Hong Chang"}, {"authorId": "2250286882", "name": "Shiguang Shan"}, {"authorId": "2328078666", "name": "Cheng-Lin Wu"}], "abstract": "Research idea innovation is a fundamental engine of scientific progress, yet it remains difficult to generate and evaluate in a scalable and controllable way. This challenge lies in its inherently open-ended and multi-objective nature, where ideas should balance novelty, plausibility and feasibility. While recent LLM-based approaches have made progress through carefully designed prompts or agent pipelines, they are constrained by predefined, static ideation workflows. To address this limitation, we propose MindFlow, a framework that explicitly formulates ideation as a graph-structured Flow in Mind, which is composed of modular thinking operators and modeled by a probabilistic mind supernet. Given a research topic, a controller dynamically samples thinking flows to generate candidate ideas. This open-ended problem is optimized using a tournament-based relative ranking, enabling the controller to progressively favor higher-quality thinking flows. We further introduce an evaluation protocol that jointly assesses problem finding and problem solving, going beyond title- or abstractonly judgments. Across diverse topics, MindFlow shows its superiority as an explicit, controllable and optimizable research idea innovator."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "666dd24257bba55b449f892a66272fe2d2232570", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.12002", "CorpusId": 292737884}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/666dd24257bba55b449f892a66272fe2d2232570", "title": "Agentic-TTT: Training test-time policy for test-time training", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.12002, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-08", "authors": [{"authorId": "2407452066", "name": "Jia-Hao Lu"}, {"authorId": "2288342768", "name": "Mohan S. Kankanhalli"}], "abstract": "Test-time training (TTT) adapts an LLM's parameters using signals derived from test inputs, and can make striking improvements in pre-specified settings such as IMO competitions or designated open problems. By turning deployment experience into parameter updates, TTT provides a direct mechanism for model-level self-improvement. Yet TTT is not universally beneficial: each TTT algorithm works in different settings, and applying an ill-suited method could waste test-time compute or even damage model performance. Therefore, such parameter-level self-improvement requires agency: the model must decide when TTT is warranted, which algorithm to invoke, and whether an existing skill can be reused. To fill this gap, we introduce Agentic-TTT, which learns a test-time policy to govern those decisions. Agentic-TTT turns TTT procedures into callable tools, treats accumulated skills as an evolving deployment environment, and trains its policy using the observed utility gains from its decisions. On our benchmark, Agentic-TTT nearly doubles the utility over the backbone model, learns to trade off utility against compute, and generalizes to domains unseen during training. Together, these results point toward autonomous self-improvement: models that can decide how to learn from their own deployment experience."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "781222883a8306b608949301ba4ea625cd94614b", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.11655", "CorpusId": 292737930}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/781222883a8306b608949301ba4ea625cd94614b", "title": "Harness Evolution Hits a Ceiling: When Weight Training Should Begin", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.11655, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-08", "authors": [{"authorId": "2283776098", "name": "Yuan Tian"}, {"authorId": "2385446371", "name": "Bing Hu"}, {"authorId": "2353314544", "name": "Hao Wang"}, {"authorId": "2195352476", "name": "Bing-Hang Lu"}, {"authorId": "2408941235", "name": "Fang Wu"}], "abstract": "Improving a long-horizon LLM agent means evolving the harness around a frozen model or training its weights. We let a self-evolving harness make the system stronger first, then cross seed and evolved harnesses with base and trained weights to learn which gains the trained model keeps and which still need the runtime. We show that the right lever can be read off the agent's failure composition: labelling failed trajectories by the first signal that fires separates process failures (blocked calls, loops, exhausted step budgets) from content failures (a delivered plan that is poor). Harness evolution repairs the former, the behaviour it instils can be trained into the weights, and content failures are what weight training is for. On DeepPlanning, a self-evolving harness loop lifts the held-out score of Qwen3.5-4B from 0.16 to 0.30 and of Qwen3.5-9B from 0.32 to 0.44; for 4B, held-out delivery rises from 55% to 90% while content failures are left for the weights. LoRA adapters trained on evolved-harness trajectories internalise the gain: under the original harness they add +0.13 on held-out tasks for both sizes; on 4B they stack with the harness to more than double the held-out score, and on 9B the adapter alone matches the full evolution line, cutting content failures from a quarter of trajectories to one in twenty. A placebo adapter trained on answer-shuffled trajectories falls below the base model. The loop transfers to WebArena-Lite (+0.09 on 117 unseen tasks), where the gain lives in what the model sees and adapters do not add to it. The result is a diagnose-then-intervene rule applied twice: read the failure composition to choose between harness and weights, then read what the accepted edits changed to decide which gains to train in. Scores are four-rollout means against fresh anchors, same-night except where marked, across eight models from six families and two benchmarks."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "6116dcf16c15992d5439b93a4fbec5db1224ecf1", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.12176", "CorpusId": 292738246}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6116dcf16c15992d5439b93a4fbec5db1224ecf1", "title": "Recursive Self-Improvement through Multi-Agent Self-Supervision", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.12176, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-08", "authors": [{"authorId": "2110007977", "name": "Hyunin Lee"}, {"authorId": "2223017366", "name": "Jinglue Xu"}, {"authorId": "2360171459", "name": "Jeffrey Seely"}, {"authorId": "2263095684", "name": "Donghyun Lee"}, {"authorId": "2363570697", "name": "Somayeh Sojoudi"}, {"authorId": "2257206424", "name": "Matei A. Zaharia"}, {"authorId": "2326422916", "name": "Yu-Jin Tang"}], "abstract": "Recursive self-improvement (RSI) of a model on non-verifiable tasks, such as open-ended research, faces a supervision bottleneck when its outputs exceed what even human experts can reliably assess, leaving the model itself (optimizee) as the best available optimizer and evaluator. However, a single model instance struggles to critique and improve its own complex reasoning under this homogeneous loop. To address this, we propose Multi-Agent Self-Supervision (MASS), an RSI method that alternates between evolutionary workflow optimization and supervised fine-tuning on self-generated trajectories. Guided by early findings that multi-agent topologies excel at complex reasoning, MASS prompts a single base model to iteratively propose, execute, and self-evaluate multi-agent workflows. Through an evolutionary search constrained by structural guardrails, the model optimizes these computational-graph-like orchestrations, discovering the most effective distinct roles and information routing for a given task. Over two MASS cycles with Qwen3.6-27B, the model achieves 1.2-1.6x higher performance per output tokens on four open-ended public benchmarks. Because the improved model subsequently acts as a better optimizer and evaluator, this alternating framework enables a continuous, recursive bootstrapping of the model's capabilities. Moreover, multi-agent traces are also more training-efficient: a student trained on them outperforms a single-agent student trained on 1.4x more training tokens. These findings suggest that jointly learning orchestration and bounded subagent execution from multi-agent trajectories can provide an effective signal for RSI."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "54696b645d433f18c2825145757a6406c665bdf9", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.09684", "CorpusId": 292700644}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/54696b645d433f18c2825145757a6406c665bdf9", "title": "From Pareto to Preference: Personalized Test-Time Scaling via Amortized Agentic Policy Discovery", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.09684, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-07", "authors": [{"authorId": "2254837328", "name": "Xing-Lin Wang"}, {"authorId": "2479386655", "name": "Zi-Shen Liu"}, {"authorId": "2328084495", "name": "Tong Zheng"}, {"authorId": "1519472504", "name": "Shaoxiong Feng"}, {"authorId": "2253609260", "name": "Pei-Wen Yuan"}, {"authorId": "2275295939", "name": "Yi-Wei Li"}, {"authorId": "2256650286", "name": "Jia-Yi Shi"}, {"authorId": "2318565828", "name": "Yue-Qi Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2319243853", "name": "Chu-Yi Tan"}, {"authorId": "2347555330", "name": "Ji Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2275135250", "name": "Bo-Yuan Pan"}, {"authorId": "2253855196", "name": "Kan Li"}], "abstract": "Test-time scaling (TTS) improves the reasoning capabilities of large language models by allocating additional inference computation. Existing approaches to improving TTS efficiency largely optimize accuracy against one resource dimension at a time, advancing either the accuracy--cost or accuracy--latency Pareto frontier. Yet user requirements are multidimensional: users may specify accuracy, latency, and inference-cost requirements jointly, and different requirements can favor different controllers. We formulate Personalized Test-Time Scaling as discovering executable controllers that maximize the joint satisfaction rate of user-specific requirements. To reduce the overhead of repeated policy discovery for new user profiles, we propose PersonTTS, an amortized agentic policy-discovery framework that reuses prior search experience through requirement-matched controller initialization and source-distilled procedural guidance, while retaining target-profile evaluation for every candidate. Experiments on AIME and HMMT show that PersonTTS substantially outperforms strong TTS baselines in joint requirement satisfaction on unseen user profiles and held-out problems. Under the same candidate-evaluation budget, cross-user experience reuse further improves policy quality while substantially reducing discovery-agent time and cost."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "da804455e88545fa82cbadc0d0a5abd9487d0051", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.07851", "CorpusId": 292663167}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/da804455e88545fa82cbadc0d0a5abd9487d0051", "title": "RA-MoWE: Workflow-Affinity Embeddings for Query Clustering and Agentic Workflow Generation", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.07851, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-06", "authors": [{"authorId": "2361705246", "name": "Qi Cheng"}, {"authorId": "2384046089", "name": "Sheng-Yu Chen"}, {"authorId": "2283249309", "name": "Wei Cheng"}, {"authorId": "2259594433", "name": "Yi-Qun Xie"}, {"authorId": "2256769706", "name": "Hao-Yu Wang"}, {"authorId": "2204622281", "name": "Hai-Feng Chen"}, {"authorId": "2260648707", "name": "Xiao-Wei Jia"}], "abstract": "Agentic workflows enable large language models (LLMs) to solve complex tasks by coordinating reasoning, tool use, and verification. However, a workflow optimized for an entire task collection can overlook differences in the reasoning strategies that individual queries need, while searching for a new workflow for every query repeats costly optimization. To address this tradeoff, we introduce RA-MoWE, a framework that uses workflow-affinity embeddings to cluster queries and guide the generation of reusable expert workflows. Each embedding records how well a fixed set of reference workflows solves a query, revealing similarities in which reasoning strategies are effective. RA-MoWE uses each cluster's queries and average embedding to initialize and refine a specialized workflow through execution feedback. An embedding encoder predicts these embeddings from query text, allowing new queries to select a generated expert without first executing the reference workflows. On a 300-query test set drawn from four benchmarks spanning mathematics, science, and programming, RA-MoWE improves average task score by 4.04 percentage points over selecting among the reference workflows, while using 27.7% fewer language-model calls at inference."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "0356a626f65600784db6320f13f01f802729035d", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.08647", "CorpusId": 292662504}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/0356a626f65600784db6320f13f01f802729035d", "title": "SquidAgent: Parallelize Wisely, Coordinate Efficiently", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.08647, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-06", "authors": [{"authorId": "2288299079", "name": "Ye-Xiong Lin"}, {"authorId": "2367227017", "name": "Shan-Shan Ye"}, {"authorId": "2313702049", "name": "Yu Yao"}, {"authorId": "2260507363", "name": "Zhen Fang"}, {"authorId": "2257285258", "name": "Bo Han"}, {"authorId": "2334506046", "name": "Tong-Liang Liu"}], "abstract": "LLM-based agents solve complex multi-step tasks, but sequential execution incurs substantial latency. In principle, parallelizing work across multiple agents should yield near-linear speedups. Yet existing parallel multi-agent systems often run slower than a single-agent baseline. We attribute this gap to two hidden costs that parallel execution incurs but a serial agent avoids. First, there is a re-exploration cost: redundant effort spent by parallel workers reconstructing context that the orchestrator already possesses, such as prior decisions, that would otherwise be inherited implicitly in a serial execution. Second, there is an alignment cost: the overhead required to reconcile inconsistencies across independently generated outputs. We thus derive a principled decision criterion: a layer should be parallelized only when its critical-path cost, plus re-exploration and alignment overheads, is lower than the corresponding serial cost. While this criterion is naturally expressed in wall-clock time, we observe that LLMs are poorly calibrated when asked to estimate task duration. To address this, we instead measure cost in predicted output tokens, which we empirically find LLMs can estimate substantially more reliably than wall-clock time. Building on this token-based criterion, we propose SquidAgent. It estimates all token budgets in a single planning step, forks each worker directly from the orchestrator's session to eliminate re-exploration cost, and replaces post-hoc reconciliation with a pre-generated shared convention block that converts alignment into a bounded upfront cost. A deterministic scheduler then applies the criterion layer by layer. Empirically, SquidAgent achieves a 2.2$\\times$ mean throughput improvement and a 2.6$\\times$ mean wall-time speedup over Claude Code, and a 2.0$\\times$ throughput improvement over the strongest multi-agent baseline."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "8dbffc42d38eee0e78e6d61bca5ebe1aca267c8e", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.08621", "CorpusId": 292663763}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/8dbffc42d38eee0e78e6d61bca5ebe1aca267c8e", "title": "Recursive Game Creator: An Agentic Product-Level Experience-Oriented Game Harness", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.08621, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-06", "authors": [{"authorId": "2381921380", "name": "Jiajun Chen"}, {"authorId": "2351231497", "name": "Hao-Yu Wu"}, {"authorId": "2334744885", "name": "Ming-Da Jia"}, {"authorId": "2233227802", "name": "Xi-Hui Liu"}], "abstract": "Recent game design agents have made substantial progress in generating playable games. However, program correctness does not ensure an enjoyable experience for players. We present Recursive Game Creator, an experience-oriented harness to advance agentic game development from rough game prototypes into entertaining games. Recursive Game Creator organizes recursive development around four components: Designer, Builder, Player, and Reviewer. The Designer translates user instructions and Reviewer's feedback into detailed plans. The Builder turns these plans into candidate games. The coding-native Player creates and executes reusable policies through programmatic interfaces to efficiently collect diverse gameplay trajectories, mitigating evaluation bias caused by slow GUI-based collection. The Reviewer uses carefully designed trajectory-based metrics to induce player preferences, integrating with visual evidence and explicit textual preferences to evaluate games against game-specific criteria. Finally, the Reviewer accepts the better version and provides improvement reviews for the next round, closing the recursive loop. Our method achieves state-of-the-art overall performance of 77.89 on GameCraft-Bench. On GameASG-Bench, it achieves a strict task success rate of 53.2%, a 34.1% improvement over the same-model baseline, and the highest mean runtime-check pass rate at 93.4% among compared methods. A user study shows longer playtime and higher ratings. Project page is at: https://imbaldy.github.io/recursive-game-creator/."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "7142392d13adc9ff400e2127e5fd09693626b83d", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.07556", "CorpusId": 292663720}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/7142392d13adc9ff400e2127e5fd09693626b83d", "title": "Decoupled Multi-Agent Orchestration", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.07556, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-06", "authors": [{"authorId": "1716206822", "name": "Xin-Le Wu"}, {"authorId": "2404206797", "name": "Yao Lu"}], "abstract": "Learned orchestration can automatically construct effective language-model multi-agent systems, but existing approaches couple planning to fixed worker pools and train decomposition and collaboration from the same terminal outcome, limiting transfer and obscuring credit assignment. We introduce DeOrch, which separates worker-agnostic planning from concrete worker selection. Its two-stage planner first decomposes the task without worker information, then chooses collaboration operations using compact, worker-identity-free matchability feedback from the pool, enabling conditional credit assignment to decomposition and collaboration decisions. A lightweight matcher estimates worker suitability from behavior on a fixed probe set and adapts online with a contextual bandit, allowing new workers to be incorporated without retraining the planner or matcher. Across diverse in- and out-of-distribution tasks, DeOrch outperforms prior automatic MAS orchestration methods with fewer worker calls than competing learned orchestrators, remains effective when transferred to an entirely unseen worker pool without retraining, and shows consistent gains from both components."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "e27ae00aade4a6ed0c94b4a8c0ed169ae2006603", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.09197", "CorpusId": 292694730}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/e27ae00aade4a6ed0c94b4a8c0ed169ae2006603", "title": "FreeEvolve: Learning to Evolve Beyond Fixed Loops", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.09197, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-06", "authors": [{"authorId": "2164063663", "name": "Lecheng Kong"}, {"authorId": "2456704427", "name": "Li-Ke Hui"}, {"authorId": "2273556743", "name": "Nikos Kanakaris"}, {"authorId": "47487418", "name": "Prithwish Jana"}, {"authorId": "2325953477", "name": "Sahika Genc"}, {"authorId": "2074296975", "name": "Narayanan Sadagopan"}], "abstract": "Agent evolvers automate the design of the prompts, skills and workflows around language model agents, yet the optimization process they follow is still designed by hand: a fixed search loop decides how candidates are evaluated, which are kept and when the search stops. We propose FREEEVOLVE, which automates this process as well. An environment specifies the goal, target agent, evaluator, data and resource limits; within these limits, the evolver itself decides what to test, how much evidence to collect, which candidates to pursue and when to stop. These decisions follow an editable evolution skill, which we improve through meta-evolution by scoring each candidate skill on the fresh target agent it produces. The optimization process thus becomes a capability learned from experience rather than a loop engineered in advance. On tau3-bench, ARC-AGI-2, ARC-AGI-3 and Terminal-Bench 2.1, FREEEVOLVE controls the evolution campaign by itself, yet improves the primary held-out metric by 13.6 points on average and matches or exceeds hand-designed evolvers. The learned process keeps improving with experience: meta-evolved skills add 6.9 points over the seed skill on fresh target agents, demonstrating transferability across environments."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "a083e479510bc34d8ba5f1f3706a858432e202e8", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.05923", "CorpusId": 292655286}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/a083e479510bc34d8ba5f1f3706a858432e202e8", "title": "VERA: Scaling Verifiable Environments for Agentic co-Evolution", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.05923, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-05", "authors": [{"authorId": "2409174215", "name": "Jun-Qi Liu"}, {"authorId": "2505112633", "name": "Yong-Yang Pan"}, {"authorId": "2222322049", "name": "Zhuo-Song Jiang"}, {"authorId": "2319217568", "name": "Dong-Bai Li"}, {"authorId": "2502206556", "name": "Bo Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2453994938", "name": "Xi-Tong Ling"}, {"authorId": "2502613093", "name": "Sheng Wang"}, {"authorId": "2372190732", "name": "Han-Rong Ye"}, {"authorId": "2255499417", "name": "Yu-Fan He"}, {"authorId": "2269185997", "name": "Can Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2305603785", "name": "Peng-Fei Guo"}, {"authorId": "2309095535", "name": "Dong Yang"}, {"authorId": "2611242", "name": "Andriy Myronenko"}, {"authorId": "2306069928", "name": "Yu-Yin Zhou"}, {"authorId": "2507869942", "name": "Tian-Yu Liu"}, {"authorId": "2333986617", "name": "Da-Guang Xu"}, {"authorId": "46556781", "name": "Yu-Cheng Tang"}], "abstract": "Competent agents need precise and verifiable environments, such as sandboxes that are resumable at any stage and evolve from observable evidence. However, most long-horizon work exposes how rare these are: for example, an agent in medical research must ground a finding, classify it, and write a report over dozens of dependent steps, yet recent environments score only the outcome. To address the challenges in stable training, we present VERA, which builds such environments at scale and lets agents evolve on them. VERA builds these environments from initial trajectories: an agent writes rubrics, executable checks, a judge verifies each sandbox, and only those that pass enter the training bank. On these environments, VERA alternates between two updates: train the model with rubric rewards, or edit the harness skills. We also create a verifier which gates model checkpoints and harness edits using explicit development-set acceptance criteria. This attribution distinguishes VERA's co-evolution from single-axis baselines: its updates target not only the cause but the outcome. With an open-source corpus of 9,000+ long-horizon verifiable environments, a 9B model paired with its co-evolved agent beats the strongest baseline by 10.3 and 13.0 points in the two domains. At 27B, it surpasses the baseline on AutoCoWorkBench (71.6) and AutoMedBench (80.7), transfers to unseen workflows, and retains general capabilities."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "971a28804a3348d9e4bfe9ca6417d0bc072ad01a", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.05284", "CorpusId": 292655213}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/971a28804a3348d9e4bfe9ca6417d0bc072ad01a", "title": "Fusion is the New Mutation: Bandit-Guided Evolution on Workflow Graphs", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.05284, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-04", "authors": [{"authorId": "2382918145", "name": "Zhi-Wei Shang"}, {"authorId": "2343681730", "name": "Jia-Hang Sun"}, {"authorId": "2507053673", "name": "Ming-Rong Gong"}, {"authorId": "2343658051", "name": "Ming-Ze Kong"}, {"authorId": "2367194458", "name": "Zi-Kun Qu"}, {"authorId": "2436752563", "name": "Ping-Chen Lu"}, {"authorId": "2450154516", "name": "Jun Dong"}, {"authorId": "2507857055", "name": "Zhi-Piao Liu"}, {"authorId": "2507863263", "name": "Hong-Wei Yang"}, {"authorId": "2507864943", "name": "Guo-Qing Xie"}, {"authorId": "2382919045", "name": "Yao Shu"}, {"authorId": "2346815014", "name": "Zhong-Xiang Dai"}], "abstract": "Automated agentic workflow optimization relies on costly evaluations, making it essential to allocate a limited evaluation budget effectively. Multi-parent fusion can reuse designs from previously discovered workflows, but identifying promising parent combinations requires learning from limited fusion feedback. We introduce DAGO (Directed Acyclic Graph Optimization), a contextual-bandit-guided framework that learns which parent workflows to fuse under a limited evaluation budget. DAGO formulates each candidate parent combination as an arm, represented by pretrained embeddings of its constituent workflows'code and prompts. A diagonal LinUCB policy learns a shared reward model across arms and balances exploitation of arms with high predicted offspring quality against uncertainty-driven exploration. After an arm is selected, an LLM generates a child workflow through summary-guided fusion, and the child's validation score serves as the reward for updating the bandit. A shared directed acyclic graph maintains discovered workflows and their multi-parent lineage, providing an expanding pool of parents for subsequent arm proposals. Across six benchmarks covering mathematical reasoning, code generation, and question answering, DAGO achieves the highest macro-average score among the evaluated baselines. Under matched validation-evaluation budgets, it improves over AFlow from 80.3 to 81.7 while reducing aggregate search expenditure by 11.2%. Ablation studies show that LinUCB-guided arm selection outperforms both random selection and its exploration-free variant, supporting the value of feedback-driven selection and exploration-exploitation balance."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "316743c076e1bb5ef0e1db242e6f189f46d8bb50", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.05382", "CorpusId": 292655845}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/316743c076e1bb5ef0e1db242e6f189f46d8bb50", "title": "Harness-Search: Guiding Long-Horizon Search through Multi-Agent Coordination", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.05382, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-04", "authors": [{"authorId": "2363592558", "name": "Shanyong Wang"}, {"authorId": "2505512564", "name": "Zhen-Wen Ji"}, {"authorId": "2394337450", "name": "Lei Jin"}, {"authorId": "2368724514", "name": "Yi-Ning Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2504985711", "name": "Yi-Chen Qian"}, {"authorId": "2305654807", "name": "Cheng-Qiang Lu"}, {"authorId": "2379038661", "name": "Yi Wu"}, {"authorId": "2379225708", "name": "Yao Hu"}, {"authorId": "2363669598", "name": "Li-Zhen Cui"}, {"authorId": "2110198892", "name": "Yan-Yu Xu"}], "abstract": "Long-horizon search requires agents to gather evidence across multiple steps and synthesize it into well-supported answers. The recent agent harnesses provide a natural and promising framework to support such long-running search processes. As interaction histories grow, one single agent in harnesses might get stuck and cause the policy to lose track of unresolved questions, overlook useful evidence, or terminate before sufficient support has been collected. One of promising way is to decouple three distinct responsibilities of proposing retrieval actions, updating persistent state, and deciding when to stop rather than concentrating them within a single policy. Targeted at it, we introduce Harness-Search, a multi-agent search harness to reduce the local errors propagating across subsequent exploration, evidence curation, and termination decisions. In particular, Harness-Search assigns these responsibilities to three permission-bounded authorities: a Retrieval Policy that proposes search operations, a Memory Operator that validates and commits persistent-state updates, and a Summary Auditor that accepts or rejects termination based on the sufficiency of the curated evidence. Together, these roles form a Propose-Commit-Audit loop in which actions are proposed, persistent evidence is selectively committed, and stopping decisions are subjected to an explicit sufficiency check. Across seven long-horizon search benchmarks, Harness-Search improves both retrieval and answer generation under the same policy backbone, increasing Recall by 4.60-27.92 points and Final-Answer Recall by 12.34-30.13 points over the strongest harness-based baseline on each evidence-retrieval benchmark. Moreover, trajectory-level analyses show that Harness-Search continues to accumulate useful evidence and expand evidence coverage with less redundant retrieval as the search history grows."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "6e8ab9099806297366bd73edb029f86c8b131e30", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.04991", "CorpusId": 292654250}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/6e8ab9099806297366bd73edb029f86c8b131e30", "title": "CIPO: Counterfactual Imagination Policy Optimization for Adaptive Tool Granularity Selection", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.04991, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-04", "authors": [{"authorId": "2407503634", "name": "Yu Li"}, {"authorId": "2507842071", "name": "Y. Wan"}, {"authorId": "2507856580", "name": "Zi-Jian Zhu"}, {"authorId": "2357857537", "name": "Han-Jun Luo"}, {"authorId": "2499870793", "name": "Chao-Qian Ren"}, {"authorId": "2507485571", "name": "Long-Fei Li"}, {"authorId": "2329336949", "name": "Lei Feng"}], "abstract": "Large language model (LLM) agents solve complex tasks through multi-step interactions with external tools. These interactions often contain recurring local tool sequences. Treating such sequences as composite\"Skills\"can shorten tool-use trajectories and reduce repeated low-level decisions. However, when atomic tools and composite skills coexist, skill use becomes a policy problem: the agent must decide whether the current state requires atomic fine control or skill-level abstraction. In this paper, we argue that effective skill use should be studied as adaptive tool granularity selection. The most direct training signal for this problem is to compare the consequences of atomic and skill choices available from the same state. Based on this view, we propose CIPO, a Counterfactual Imagination Policy Optimization framework for adaptive tool granularity. CIPO constructs executable skills through budget-constrained mining of successful tool-use trajectories and trains granularity decisions with counterfactual branch rollouts. For each base rollout, CIPO branches at the first eligible granularity decision and replaces the chosen action with a feasible atomic or skill alternative. The paired outcome difference serves as a supplementary reward for policy optimization. Experiments across multiple benchmarks and model backbones show that CIPO improves task success and decision efficiency over baselines. Further analyses show that CIPO learns effective skill use by improving the choice between atomic tools and composite skills based on the current state, without simply increasing skill frequency."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "32e544e9bd6160db13f9eef67158b4615dbc995c", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.05039", "CorpusId": 292655502}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/32e544e9bd6160db13f9eef67158b4615dbc995c", "title": "Causal Improvement Graph for Agentic Harness Optimization", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.05039, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-04", "authors": [{"authorId": "2350521366", "name": "Jun-Jie Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2128786021", "name": "Shunyu Liu"}, {"authorId": "2363879148", "name": "Hao-Yu Wang"}, {"authorId": "2303419056", "name": "Ting-En Lin"}, {"authorId": "2237932828", "name": "Yong-Bin Li"}, {"authorId": "2354552882", "name": "Da-Cheng Tao"}], "abstract": "Agentic Harness is the runtime that constructs task context and controls execution flow, thereby shaping overall agent performance. Given a fixed model and external evaluation, automated Harness optimization seeks to improve this runtime through an iterative proposal--evaluation loop to better solve target tasks. Existing meta-harness methods mainly adopt proposer-centric discovery, in which an LLM-based proposer integrates accumulated experimental findings to determine subsequent Harness revisions. This places the burden of maintaining the evolving improvement state on the proposer as history expands and its underlying experimental logic becomes harder to discern. In this paper, we introduce the Causal Improvement Graph (CIG), a graph-governed meta-harness framework that externalizes the evolving improvement state in a persistent graph, allowing prior findings to directly govern subsequent Harness optimization through local proposer operations. CIG grows and links Evidence, Hypothesis, Intervention, and Outcome nodes to represent what was observed, how it may be explained, how to test that explanation, and what the evaluation reveals. Their structural relations preserve how the improvement state changes across iterations, allowing local proposers to build directly on relations among prior findings rather than recover them from raw history. Across various agent tasks, CIG discovers stronger Harnesses than previous meta-harness baselines and remains robust to the choice of task solver and proposer. Structural ablations further support the design of an explicit improvement state with graph-governed evolution."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "ca9b2105ffc695eff61a5c519d1ee5c6d9ea9589", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.04178", "CorpusId": 292638871}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/ca9b2105ffc695eff61a5c519d1ee5c6d9ea9589", "title": "SHarP: Saliency-based Pruning of Agent Harnesses", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.04178, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-03", "authors": [{"authorId": "2218539063", "name": "Xinyi Gao"}, {"authorId": "2112250365", "name": "Qiucheng Wu"}, {"authorId": "9566679", "name": "Kai-Zhi Qian"}, {"authorId": "2286357082", "name": "Han-Dong Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2191955409", "name": "Shi-Yu Chang"}, {"authorId": "2319131554", "name": "Yang Zhang"}], "abstract": "Agent harnesses are systems that coordinate model calls, tool use, and task execution to help large language models complete complex tasks. To meet task requirements and address failures, these systems are often iteratively refined by amending and patching their instructions, tools, and workflows, continuously increasing harness complexity. It is therefore unclear whether some resulting harness modules are redundant, introducing substantial token overhead with little, if any, performance gain. Inspired by neural network pruning, in this paper, we study harness pruning as a means of striking a better balance between task performance and token cost. We propose SHarP (Saliency-based Harness Pruning), a simple yet effective pruning strategy based on the saliency of each harness module with respect to performance and efficiency. Specifically, we first identify tools, instructions, and supporting mechanisms as components that can be individually disabled. We then estimate the saliency of each module by ablating it and assessing its task performance and token cost relative to the full set of single-module ablations. Modules with the smallest contribution to performance or largest computational overhead are subsequently pruned. Our evaluation across various harnesses on held-out validation sets reveals a surprising finding: most harnesses that we studied are highly redundant and can maintain comparable performance and efficiency even after a substantial portion of their modules are pruned. Our pruning approach and empirical findings provide new perspectives on agent harness design and optimization."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "429b60d65837358e55206769186fa575002ef370", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.02951", "CorpusId": 292587740}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/429b60d65837358e55206769186fa575002ef370", "title": "Dynamic Expert Pruning for Multi-Agent Systems", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.02951, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-02", "authors": [{"authorId": "2328309591", "name": "Jabin Koo"}, {"authorId": "117181261", "name": "Soheil Abbasloo"}, {"authorId": "2339716370", "name": "Sungjae Lee"}, {"authorId": "2525635", "name": "Jungseul Ok"}], "abstract": "Mixture-of-Experts (MoE) architectures scale language models efficiently by activating only a few experts per token, but the saving is confined to computation: every expert must stay resident on the accelerator, so memory bounds where these models can be deployed. Expert pruning reduces this footprint, yet existing methods are static --- a single mask, calibrated offline, is applied to the model for every subsequent request. This assumption can fail when the workload is heterogeneous, most prominently in multi-agent systems, where one backbone serves many tasks and roles at once: our analysis shows that different tasks and roles recruit different experts, while static methods assign one fixed subset to all of them. We therefore propose Dynamic Expert Pruning (DEP), which rests on a finding we establish here: an agent's system and task prompts are by themselves sufficient to identify the experts that agent and its task require, since that text already describes what the agent will do. A lightweight predictor, trained once on workflow transcripts, turns those prompts into a specialized per-request mask in a single forward pass, with no per-configuration calibration. Across diverse tasks and roles, model scales, and MoE architectures, DEP achieves better overall accuracy than static pruning and merging baselines, and generalizes to workflows unseen in training without retraining. Its margin over those baselines is largest when few experts are retained, suggesting that the role specialization inherent to multi-agent systems permits sparser serving than static pruning allows."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "95cbbdba1638cd57d440ae22bffdc201e6dd3866", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.02920", "CorpusId": 292587143}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/95cbbdba1638cd57d440ae22bffdc201e6dd3866", "title": "HASTE: Evolving Agent Harnesses Against Emerging Attacks Using Sparse Evidence", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.02920, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-02", "authors": [{"authorId": "2402491320", "name": "Xi-Qiao Xiong"}, {"authorId": "2118769749", "name": "Mo-Xin Li"}, {"authorId": "2234507850", "name": "Zhi-Xin Ma"}, {"authorId": "2211602955", "name": "Ouxiang Li"}, {"authorId": "2408862789", "name": "Wen-Jie Wang"}, {"authorId": "2311512594", "name": "Fu-Li Feng"}, {"authorId": "2239071206", "name": "Xiang-Nan He"}], "abstract": "Agent harnesses play a critical role in defenses by enforcing safety constraints to prevent unsafe actions. However, rapidly emerging attacks outpace manual harness adaptation, motivating automated harness evolution. Yet the signals available for harness evolution are often sparse, such as brief descriptions or a few attack examples in threat reports and preprints. To address this limitation, we introduce HASTE, a multi-agent framework that evolves agent harnesses from sparse threat evidence through an adversarial interplay between safety-specification generation and attack-case generation. Safety specifications guide harness updates toward addressing identified safety vulnerabilities, while attack cases probe for remaining safety vulnerabilities after each update. By feeding evaluation outcomes back into both processes, HASTE enables harness evolution against emerging attacks beyond the initially observed evidence. Experimental results across multiple backbone models, attack types, and evidence forms show that HASTE consistently reduces attack success rates while preserving benign-task utility. The code is available at https://github.com/xxiqiao/HASTE."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "ed18460ff833bbcecb34274a2b9a12488ff25355", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.03548", "CorpusId": 292590372}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/ed18460ff833bbcecb34274a2b9a12488ff25355", "title": "Recursive Harness Self-Improvement for Frontier Reasoning Data Synthesis", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.03548, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-02", "authors": [{"authorId": "2328428018", "name": "Wen-Long Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2382925293", "name": "Zheng-Bo Jiao"}, {"authorId": "2507754390", "name": "Chen-Xu Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2148649304", "name": "Lekang Jiang"}, {"authorId": "2505395119", "name": "Si-Yuan Ma"}, {"authorId": "2439330712", "name": "Qi-Tuan Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2348972082", "name": "Guo Chen"}, {"authorId": "2362426022", "name": "Lin-Feng Zhang"}], "abstract": "Generating progressively harder reasoning problems requires synthesis procedures that adapt as the task distribution evolves. Existing task-level recursion reuses generated problems as seeds but leaves the construction harness unchanged. We present task-harness co-evolution, a framework for recursive harness self-improvement (RSI) in reasoning-data synthesis. Online self-improvement converts intermediate solver failures into reusable skills during generation. Post-task self-improvement revises skills, prompts, and workflows after each batch, adopting candidates only when they generate harder valid tasks within a bounded cost increase. Model weights and verification criteria remain fixed. Across mathematics, coding, and science, mean solver accuracy decreases from 100.0% to 54.8% over fourteen evolution rounds. Ablations show that combining both update schedules produces harder tasks than fixed-harness recursion or either schedule alone. The resulting data improves downstream SFT and GRPO performance. In particular, a 27B student fine-tuned on 10K synthesized mathematics examples achieves 62.5% mean-16 accuracy on APEX, competitive with selected frontier-model references. These results support adapting the synthesis harness alongside the tasks to generate increasingly challenging data with downstream training value."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "34467fe773c238ee2d331d6553ff950c3b6bcb51", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.04137", "CorpusId": 292639413}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/34467fe773c238ee2d331d6553ff950c3b6bcb51", "title": "Dynamic Harness Search: Building Multi-Agent Systems Per-Query via Prediction", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.04137, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-02", "authors": [{"authorId": "2305681858", "name": "Som Sagar"}, {"authorId": "2505801877", "name": "Sha-Sha Li"}, {"authorId": "2393382483", "name": "He-Jie Cui"}, {"authorId": "1388248711", "name": "Ransalu Senanayake"}, {"authorId": "2676352", "name": "Sercan \u00d6. Arik"}], "abstract": "Agent harnesses specify the roles, instructions, tools, and communication structure used to solve a task, and the right harness depends on the query. Because the value of each design choice is observable only through execution, tailoring a harness to each query has required either executing alternatives at inference time or costly manual design. We introduce SHIFT, which moves execution out of the per-query search loop. A local LLM architect learns a policy over harness-building actions from search, and a value function that predicts, from measured executions, a utility balancing accuracy against execution cost. For each query, Monte Carlo tree search uses these predictions to construct a harness. Across 9,193 tasks in six benchmarks, from math to document and general-assistant tasks, with a Gemini 3.5 Flash executor, SHIFT attains the highest mean accuracy, about 80%, outperforming 17 baselines that span prompting, prompt optimization, and workflow search, and exceeding the strongest baseline by 7.2 percentage points. A cheaper mode of SHIFT also attains a higher mean accuracy than every baseline while using 32% fewer execution tokens than the strongest baseline. We further show that choosing structure, instructions, and tools jointly beats choosing only instructions or only tools by up to 9.1 percentage points, and that learned value selection identifies more accurate harnesses with lower execution cost from candidate pools."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "3d79b6e181bac9e2c80610d3205d77dbeb327a34", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.01073", "CorpusId": 292521530}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/3d79b6e181bac9e2c80610d3205d77dbeb327a34", "title": "Safety Must Survive Self-Improvement: Why Failures Persist and How Agents Recover", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.01073, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-01", "authors": [{"authorId": "2221081346", "name": "Yun-Bei Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2221231969", "name": "Janet Wang"}, {"authorId": "2214534099", "name": "Saiyue Lyu"}, {"authorId": "152988336", "name": "Ying-Qiang Ge"}, {"authorId": "2087743517", "name": "Kaiqu Liang"}, {"authorId": "2278326150", "name": "Zi-Jian Jin"}, {"authorId": "2322749934", "name": "Chandan K. Reddy"}, {"authorId": "2282741360", "name": "J. Hamm"}], "abstract": "Recursive self-improvement (RSI) allows agents to carry useful changes across generations. Maintaining safety across these generations involves both preventing unsafe behavior from persisting and enabling recovery when failures occur. We study these challenges through a controlled testbed of stateful authorization tasks, where fixed LLM editors optimize executable agent components and independent traces record their effects. Paired interventions separate which revisions pass validation, which program continues running, and which program the editor revises next. After a new authorization dependency invalidates previously tested optimizations, historical scores preserve the same unsafe programs in 22 of 48 framework histories despite a correct alternative in every affected archive. Refreshing scores restores correctness on the original suite, with residual failures on independently composed tests. Failures also persist under an unchanged contract when all proposals are rejected and the failed incumbent remains active. Starting from shared failures, editing the initial implementation instead of the failed one improves recovery, although the advantage varies across editors. Full validation and validated rollback end with fully correct programs in the core trajectory study while retaining over 43% deployment savings. Preserving agent safety requires checking what will run under current conditions and choosing which implementation to edit next."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "b01e154957b584cecea32b5ceabae89ddcd7a4f8", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.02001", "CorpusId": 292525312}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/b01e154957b584cecea32b5ceabae89ddcd7a4f8", "title": "Mingbird: A Local-First Agent Harness Enabling Small Open Models to Complete Real Tasks", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.02001, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-01", "authors": [{"authorId": "2506681013", "name": "Hao Wang"}, {"authorId": "2489803684", "name": "Ting-Bin Huang"}], "abstract": "Small open-weight models (2-9B) run on ordinary laptops, but under cloud-scale agent harnesses they rarely complete real tasks: tool prefill overflows the context, self-correction diverges, tool demonstrations loop, and tasks are silently abandoned. We present evidence, from a controlled single-machine comparison and one third-party benchmark, that a substantial share of these failures is attributable to the harness rather than the model. We introduce Mingbird, a local-first agent harness for Windows and Ollama whose ten mechanisms compensate point-by-point for small-model failure forms, three of them representative: a byte-level net-zero prefill budget, a finish gate that re-reads the task before accepting completion, and signature-level loop detection. On LRAB, a controlled comparison holding machine, models, budgets, and scoring fixed (4 harnesses $\\times$ 4 open models (2B-35B) $\\times$ 18 real tasks, deterministic artifact scoring), Mingbird reaches 0.886 overall against 0.631 (goose), 0.479 (opencode), and 0.405 (agent-mini), with all 288 cells published; on $\\tau^2$-bench (278 tasks, three arms, one protocol) it totals 0.856 against 0.791 and 0.737; and a frontier-model probe on the same 18 tasks spans 0.997 to 0.478 across harnesses, with well-formed scaffolds staying within 0.072 of each other. A leave-one-mechanism-out ablation is reported as directional only: same-night replications of the same arm move its mean by up to 0.069, the size of every nominal single-trial delta, and the one batch-matched comparison (full mechanism stack versus text re-read alone) gives the executable completion guards a paired +0.10 across three replications. The evidence carries stated limits: a self-built benchmark, a single machine, and single-trial scoring."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "98c7c158014562733667c37d919e2fcbb89c68be", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.01249", "CorpusId": 292525581}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/98c7c158014562733667c37d919e2fcbb89c68be", "title": "Revision-Aware Independent Agent Graphs for Dynamic Reasoning", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.01249, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-01", "authors": [{"authorId": "2490165069", "name": "Yang-Hao Luo"}, {"authorId": "2506405264", "name": "Selim-Antoine Lali"}, {"authorId": "2436219356", "name": "Jeremy Moebel"}, {"authorId": "2506407844", "name": "Iliass Khoutaibi"}, {"authorId": "2436212804", "name": "Ahmadou Aidara"}, {"authorId": "2350626033", "name": "Meng-Yu Wang"}], "abstract": "Conventional reasoning protocols present a fixed, preselected task, so they cannot test whether an agent propagates relevant updates, preserves unaffected work, or reconstructs a historical task binding. We therefore study \\emph{dynamic task routing}, in which an event stream revises task bindings and a system must select the document version valid at each query time before solving it. To study this problem, we repurpose six widely used benchmarks: MMLU, MMLU-Pro, MedMCQA, MATH, GPQA, and HumanEval into 31{,}119 dynamic episodes comprising 373{,}428 temporally categorized queries. This setting exposes a central trade-off: recomputing after every event wastes work, whereas unguarded reuse returns stale conclusions. We introduce the Revision-Aware Independent Agent Graph (RIAG), a bounded multi-agent policy that separates deterministic temporal resolution from task reasoning. RIAG caches solutions by immutable document identity, starts each fresh task with two unexposed attempts, and conditionally invokes audit and repair, using at most four calls per document version. On this collection, homogeneous RIAG achieves 54.24\\% joint routing-and-answer accuracy at 0.62 calls/query, compared with 32.22\\% at 18.00 calls/query for the strongest comparison method; heterogeneous RIAG reaches 49.78\\% at 0.63 calls/query."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "e5a78b5e9248ea88c66b3f09b7b29fe5940a736e", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2610.02396", "CorpusId": 292587607}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/e5a78b5e9248ea88c66b3f09b7b29fe5940a736e", "title": "Inherit-MAS: Test-Time Evolution of Multi-Agent Systems through Workflow and Execution Inheritance", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2610.02396, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-10-01", "authors": [{"authorId": "2409767795", "name": "Song-Tao Wei"}, {"authorId": "2197376439", "name": "Yi Li"}, {"authorId": "2293659373", "name": "Zhi-Chun Guo"}, {"authorId": "1887514", "name": "Bing-Zhe Li"}], "abstract": "Multi-agent systems (MAS) built from large language models coordinate specialized agents to tackle complex tasks, but effective workflows are difficult to design in advance. Test-time evolution refines workflows using execution feedback, yet broad revisions can disturb useful components, while re-executing unchanged requests can incur redundant computation. Inspired by the interplay of inheritance and selection in biological evolution, we introduce Inherit-MAS, which makes inheritance explicit at the workflow and execution levels. A meta-model first synthesizes a workflow of worker agents with declared roles, communication inputs, and tool permissions, and a separately prompted judge scores each executed candidate and diagnoses its deficiencies. In ordinary refinement rounds, \\emph{workflow inheritance} starts from the latest completed candidate, may discard removable nodes judged unhelpful, and applies a validated edit to address the diagnosed deficiency. When the new candidate executes, \\emph{execution inheritance} inherits eligible stored results only if the complete resolved request and execution context match, avoiding redundant model and tool calls. With GPT-4o-mini workers, Inherit-MAS achieves 55.4\\% completion on WorkBench and 49.7\\% joint F1 on HotpotQA FullWiki, outperforming EvoAgent, EvoMAS, and TacoMAS. With Qwen3-32B workers, it also exceeds these evolving-MAS baselines on both benchmarks. Compared with rerunning the same controller with execution inheritance disabled, execution inheritance reduces worker-token usage by 29.1\\% on WorkBench and 34.6\\% on HotpotQA, and total token usage by 5.3\\% and 18.1\\%."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "fa6f37a97ae12af343d3867bb6735d2d9d59cf7c", "externalIds": {"ArXiv": "2609.38762", "DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-38762", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.38762", "CorpusId": 292477109}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/fa6f37a97ae12af343d3867bb6735d2d9d59cf7c", "title": "Adaptive-GEPA: Make Your Harness Fit Heterogeneous Requests", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.38762, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-30", "authors": [{"authorId": "2399156405", "name": "Tian-Yu Chen"}, {"authorId": "2296023267", "name": "Yasi Zhang"}, {"authorId": "2266240014", "name": "Rui-Yi Wang"}, {"authorId": "2329112407", "name": "Xin-Ran Zhao"}, {"authorId": "2363567316", "name": "Tao-Ran Li"}, {"authorId": "2257010071", "name": "Ming-Yuan Zhou"}], "abstract": "Reflective optimizers such as GEPA improve language model prompts from execution traces and evaluator feedback; full-program extensions can also rewrite tools and control flow. In practice, a user hands the same endpoint heterogeneous requests whose effective solutions require different tools, reasoning modes, and control flow. Optimizing one shared program leaves this division of work implicit in source-code search, while optimizing a separate program per request family fixes it beforehand. We introduce Adaptive-GEPA, which learns both how to divide requests and how to solve them. It evolves a router and a library of specialist programs under one search budget. The router's instructions, each specialist's description, and its program code are plain, human-readable text, edited from feedback. To combine branches, it aligns specialists by the requests they handle and inherits descriptions together with programs. On a fixed mixture of four task families, the reported Qwen3-8B run evolves four experts without supplying family labels to the router or reflection model; its routing matches the task partition on all 651 test requests. Its family-mean test score (x100) rises from 52.6 to 70.6, compared with 62.5 for GEPA's full-program adapter and 54.0 for GRPO at a nominal budget of 18,000 scored calls. These counts do not equate total compute. Figure 1 summarizes the learning curves, final test scores, and routing agreement."}}, {"citingPaper": {"paperId": "45cab01e70db03edc3b0dd87c40ece565b58ce07", "externalIds": {"DBLP": "journals/corr/abs-2609-40169", "ArXiv": "2609.40169", "DOI": "10.48550/arXiv.2609.40169", "CorpusId": 292487508}, "url": "https://www.semanticscholar.org/paper/45cab01e70db03edc3b0dd87c40ece565b58ce07", "title": "Learning from Research: Toward Lifelong Agent Harness Evolution", "openAccessPdf": {"url": "", "status": null, "license": null, "disclaimer": "Notice: Paper or abstract available at https://arxiv.org/abs/2609.40169, which is subject to the license by the author or copyright owner provided with this content. Please go to the source to verify the license and copyright information for your use."}, "fieldsOfStudy": ["Computer Science"], "publicationDate": "2026-09-30", "authors": [{"authorId": "2347161302", "name": "Jing-Bo Yang"}, {"authorId": "51238382", "name": "Kwei-Herng Lai"}, {"authorId": "2370930375", "name": "Xiao-Wen Wang"}, {"authorId": "47282897", "name": "Y. Harari"}, {"authorId": "1718798", "name": "E. Gabrilovich"}, {"authorId": "2285866226", "name": "Shi-Yu Chang"}], "abstract": "Language agents are expected to solve increasingly complex tasks, creating a growing need for continual improvement. One promising approach is to evolve the agent harness, the software that governs tool use, memory management, and task execution, while keeping the underlying language model fixed. Recent methods automate this process by using a meta coding agent to modify the harness based on execution feedback. However, relying on that agent's existing knowledge and observed failures can restrict exploration and make adaptation reactive. Inspired by how human experts learn from the research literature for new solutions, we introduce ScholarEvolve, a framework that automatically draws on state-of-the-art research to guide harness evolution. ScholarEvolve organizes the harness evolution directions into functional modules and uses topic modeling to identify distinct improvement strategies for each module. It implements these strategies and evaluates their combinations to improve task performance. Moreover, the framework is designed to incorporate new publications over time, allowing research advances to drive proactive lifelong evolution. Experiments demonstrate improvements on AppWorld and Tau2-Bench. ScholarEvolve raises Qwen3.5-27B task goal completion from 49.6% to 63.6% on AppWorld Challenge, and raises GPT-5.4-mini pass@1 from 72.7% to 81.9% on Tau2-Bench Telecom."}}]}
